tanding\",\\n        \"classify\",\\n        \"compare\",\\n        \"connect-back\",\\n        \"diagnose-misconception\",\\n        \"explain-cause\",\\n        \"interpret\",\\n        \"orient\",\\n        \"practise-guided\",\\n        \"practise-independent\",\\n        \"sequence\"\\n      ],\\n      \"forbidden_terminology\": [],\\n      \"required_assessment_slots\": [\\n        \"check\"\\n      ],\\n      \"rules\": {\\n        \"action_must_be_from_bound_source_allowed_actions\": true,\\n        \"copy_evidence_refs_verbatim\": true,\\n        \"copy_ids_verbatim\": true,\\n        \"item_kind_is_fixed_upstream\": true,\\n        \"multiple_choice_ids_per_block\": \"0_or_1\",\\n        \"response_action_requires_approved_source\": true,\\n        \"reuse_across_blocks\": \"forbidden\",\\n        \"source_only_in_required_assessment_slots\": true,\\n        \"source_only_on_eligible_intent\": true,\\n        \"source_requires_learner_action\": true\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \"instruction\": \"Return the complete corrected TeachingPlan JSON. Change only fields required to satisfy these errors. Use only intents listed under slot_intent_policy for each slot. Treat assessment_source_policy as a closed contract: copy approved_item_id and evidence_ref strings verbatim; never construct or concatenate IDs. Bind sources only inside required_assessment_slots when that list is non-empty. Every bound source must have a learner_action whose action appears in that source\\'s allowed_actions. Do not emit an unbound response-bearing learner_action. Keep optional guided or independent practice passive when no structurally planned approved source belongs there. Remove forbidden terminology and use only allowed_evidence_refs.\",\\n    \"legality_catalogue_hash\": \"4a0fa15ad61602c85a0516f1b1ae13d2c98e52c3ab4d49623ef16a72d0258665\",\\n    \"previous_output\": {\\n      \"anchor_usage\": [\\n        {\\n          \"slot_id\": \"orient\",\\n          \"usage\": \"The ramp is introduced concretely: the same slope number is a single value that must be read for two separate messages.\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"slot_id\": \"explain\",\\n          \"usage\": \"The ramp\\'s slope number is separated into its size and its sign, each attached to the question it answers.\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"slot_id\": \"contrast\",\\n          \"usage\": \"The ramp is set beside a downward ramp and then beside a flat walkway and a vertical wall, so the boundary cases of slope = 0 and undefined slope are seen against the ordinary case.\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"slot_id\": \"confront\",\\n          \"usage\": \"A learner who says a ramp of slope -5 is \\'less steep than slope 2\\' is tested against the ramp, and the belief that zero slope and undefined slope are the same kind of line is tested against the flat walkway and the wall.\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"slot_id\": \"check\",\\n          \"usage\": \"The learner reads slope numbers, including a negative one and the boundary cases, and states what each tells about steepness and direction.\"\\n        }\\n      ],\\n      \"arc\": \"The lesson opens on a ramp whose slope number tells you two different things at once, then builds the idea that the size of the number carries steepness while the sign carries direction. It then puts pairs of slopes side by side \\\\u2014 including the boundary cases of zero and undefined \\\\u2014 so the learner sees where the rule stops working, and surfaces the belief that zero and undefined describe the same line. It closes by asking the learner to read slope numbers for a line they have not seen.\",\\n      \"misconception_focus_ids\": [\\n        \"m1\",\\n        \"m2\"\\n      ],\\n      \"sections\": [\\n        {\\n          \"blocks\": [\\n            {\\n              \"brief\": \"Present a straight ramp and ask what its slope number tells you. State that the answer is not one thing but two: how steep the ramp is, and whether you are walking up it or down it. Name the single slope value on the ramp and point out that the same value has to answer both questions. Do not yet say how size relates to steepness or how sign relates to direction.\",\\n              \"departure_reason\": null,\\n              \"evidence\": \"The objective asks what the sign and size of a slope tell, so the opening must put a single slope number in front of the learner and show it being read for two different messages, not one.\",\\n              \"evidence_refs\": [\\n                \"anchor.anchor-1\",\\n                \"lesson.objective\",\\n                \"slot.orient.purpose\"\\n              ],\\n              \"intent\": \"orient\",\\n              \"learner_action\": null,\\n              \"source_question_ids\": [],\\n              \"sourcebook_needs\": [],\\n              \"sourcebook_refs\": [],\\n              \"stimulus_dependencies\": [],\\n              \"task_mode\": \"none\"\\n            }\\n          ],\\n          \"specific_purpose\": \"Open attention on the ramp and surface what a single slope number is being asked to carry.\",\\n          \"transition\": null\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"blocks\": [\\n            {\\n              \"brief\": \"State the two-part reading of a slope number. The size of the number gives steepness: a slope of 4 is steeper than a slope of 2, because the vertical change is larger for the same horizontal step. The sign gives direction: positive means the line rises left to right, negative means it falls left to right. Keep these two readings separate and do not let a large negative number be described as steep but \\'less than\\' a small positive one. Ground the size reading in the ramp\\'s slope value and the direction reading in the same ramp.\",\\n              \"departure_reason\": null,\\n              \"evidence\": \"The objective names size and sign as two separate carriers of meaning, so this block must state both readings and keep them apart, using the ratio idea already established in prior-4 and prior-5 rather than re-deriving it.\",\\n              \"evidence_refs\": [\\n                \"lesson.objective\",\\n                \"anchor.anchor-1\",\\n                \"prior-4\",\\n                \"prior-5\",\\n                \"slot.explain.purpose\"\\n              ],\\n              \"intent\": \"explain\",\\n              \"learner_action\": null,\\n              \"source_question_ids\": [],\\n              \"sourcebook_needs\": [],\\n              \"sourcebook_refs\": [],\\n              \"stimulus_dependencies\": [],\\n              \"task_mode\": \"none\"\\n            },\\n            {\\n              \"brief\": \"Set apart the single statement to retain: size of slope tells steepness, sign of slope tells direction, and these are read from the same number. Do not add a second competing key idea, and do not yet address what happens when the slope is zero or undefined.\",\\n              \"departure_reason\": null,\\n              \"evidence\": \"The objective is a two-part claim about one number, so the block that marks the high-value statement must state both parts as one claim and leave the boundary cases for the next slot.\",\\n              \"evidence_refs\": [\\n                \"lesson.objective\",\\n                \"slot.explain.purpose\"\\n              ],\\n              \"intent\": \"emphasise\",\\n              \"learner_action\": null,\\n              \"source_question_ids\": [],\\n              \"sourcebook_needs\": [],\\n              \"sourcebook_refs\": [],\\n              \"stimulus_dependencies\": [],\\n              \"task_mode\": \"none\"\\n            }\\n          ],\\n          \"specific_purpose\": \"Separate the slope number into its two readings \\\\u2014 size for steepness, sign for direction \\\\u2014 and connect each to what was already learned about slope as a ratio.\",\\n          \"transition\": \"The learner has seen that one slope number carries two messages; now those two messages are separated and named.\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"blocks\": [\\n            {\\n              \"brief\": \"Set the ramp (positive slope) beside a second ramp that goes downward at the same steepness (negative slope of the same size) and show that the steepness is the same while the direction differs. Then set both beside a flat walkway, where the slope is zero, and a vertical wall, where the slope is undefined. Name what each case looks like and what its slope number does or does not tell you. Do not describe zero slope and undefined slope as the same kind of line.\",\\n              \"departure_reason\": null,\\n              \"evidence\": \"The contrast slot must expose the boundary of the idea, and the boundary here is exactly the pair of cases the objective names \\\\u2014 rising, falling, horizontal, vertical \\\\u2014 so the comparison must place all four side by side rather than explain them one at a time.\",\\n              \"evidence_refs\": [\\n                \"lesson.objective\",\\n                \"anchor.anchor-1\",\\n                \"m2\",\\n                \"slot.contrast.purpose\"\\n              ],\\n              \"intent\": \"compare\",\\n              \"learner_action\": null,\\n              \"source_question_ids\": [],\\n              \"sourcebook_needs\": [],\\n              \"sourcebook_refs\": [],\\n              \"stimulus_dependencies\": [],\\n              \"task_mode\": \"none\"\\n            },\\n            {\\n              \"brief\": \"Give the four cases \\\\u2014 slope 4, slope -4, slope 0, undefined slope \\\\u2014 and ask the learner to sort each into \\'rises\\', \\'falls\\', \\'horizontal\\', or \\'vertical\\' using only the slope number. Include one case with a large negative slope and one with a small positive slope so the learner must decide by direction first and steepness second. Do not supply the sorting rule again; the learner applies what the previous block showed.\",\\n              \"departure_reason\": null,\\n              \"evidence\": \"A classification task forces the learner to use direction and size as separate readings, which is the exact decision the objective requires and the exact place where m1 \\\\u2014 treating size alone \\\\u2014 would produce a wrong sort.\",\\n              \"evidence_refs\": [\\n                \"lesson.objective\",\\n                \"m1\",\\n                \"slot.contrast.purpose\"\\n              ],\\n              \"intent\": \"classify\",\\n              \"learner_action\": {\\n                \"action\": \"classify-items\",\\n                \"difficulty\": \"guided\",\\n                \"expected_evidence\": \"each slope value is placed in the category matching its sign, with 0 placed as horizontal and undefined placed as vertical\",\\n                \"purpose\": \"force the learner to read direction and steepness as separate pieces of information carried by one slope number\",\\n                \"target\": \"four slope values \\\\u2014 4, -4, 0, and undefined \\\\u2014 sorted into rises, falls, horizontal, and vertical\"\\n              },\\n              \"source_question_ids\": [],\\n              \"sourcebook_needs\": [],\\n              \"sourcebook_refs\": [],\\n              \"stimulus_dependencies\": [],\\n              \"task_mode\": \"formative\"\\n            }\\n          ],\\n          \"specific_purpose\": \"Place ordinary slopes beside the boundary cases of zero slope and undefined slope so the rule\\'s edge is visible.\",\\n          \"transition\": \"The learner now holds the size-and-sign rule; the next cases test where that rule stops applying.\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"blocks\": [\\n            {\\n              \"brief\": \"Name the belief that a bigger slope number always means a steeper line. Offer the case of slope -5 beside slope 2 and show that a learner holding the belief would say -5 is \\'less steep\\' or \\'not steep at all\\', when in fact -5 is the steeper line and simply goes downward. Show what the belief predicts and where it fails on a case, so the learner must discard the size-only reading and use sign and size together.\",\\n              \"departure_reason\": null,\\n              \"evidence\": \"m1 is a held belief with predictable confident wrong answers, so the block must surface the belief and let it fail on a case where size and sign disagree, rather than simply restating the correct rule.\",\\n              \"evidence_refs\": [\\n                \"m1\",\\n                \"lesson.objective\",\\n                \"anchor.anchor-1\",\\n                \"slot.confront.purpose\"\\n              ],\\n              \"intent\": \"diagnose-misconception\",\\n              \"learner_action\": null,\\n              \"source_question_ids\": [],\\n              \"sourcebook_needs\": [],\\n              \"sourcebook_refs\": [],\\n              \"stimulus_dependencies\": [],\\n              \"task_mode\": \"none\"\\n            },\\n            {\\n              \"brief\": \"Name the belief that zero slope and undefined slope describe the same kind of line. Offer the flat walkway and the vertical wall and show the belief predicting that both are \\'flat\\' or both \\'straight up\\', when in fact the flat walkway is horizontal and the vertical wall is vertical. Make the failure explicit so the learner must separate the two boundary cases.\",\\n              \"departure_reason\": null,\\n              \"evidence\": \"m2 is a belief that treats the two boundary cases as one, and the only way to dislodge it is to place the two cases side by side and let the belief produce a visibly wrong description.\",\\n              \"evidence_refs\": [\\n                \"m2\",\\n                \"lesson.objective\",\\n                \"slot.confront.purpose\"\\n              ],\\n              \"intent\": \"diagnose-misconception\",\\n              \"learner_action\": null,\\n              \"source_question_ids\": [],\\n              \"sourcebook_needs\": [],\\n              \"sourcebook_refs\": [],\\n              \"stimulus_dependencies\": [],\\n              \"task_mode\": \"none\"\\n            }\\n          ],\\n          \"specific_purpose\": \"Name the wrong beliefs about slope size and about zero versus undefined slope, and show each failing on a concrete case.\",\\n          \"transition\": \"The learner has seen the boundary cases; now the beliefs that misread them are named and tested.\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"blocks\": [\\n            {\\n              \"brief\": \"Present the approved item that asks the learner to read slope numbers on a new line, including a negative slope and a boundary case, and to state what each tells about steepness and direction. Use the item\\'s own content; do not add teaching or restate the rule.\",\\n              \"departure_reason\": null,\\n              \"evidence\": \"The check slot must measure the objective directly, and item i1 asks the learner to compare two ramps by slope, which requires reading both direction and steepness from the number rather than recalling the rule.\",\\n              \"evidence_refs\": [\\n                \"lesson.objective\",\\n                \"slot.check.purpose\",\\n                \"item.04c25319-eaa8-488c-9f5d-f9ee348fcdb7:04c25319-eaa8-488c-9f5d-f9ee348fcdb7:7f4fe57d-a07f-4d0a-92a0-185ffbefee02.i1\"\\n              ],\\n              \"intent\": \"check-understanding\",\\n              \"learner_action\": {\\n                \"action\": \"select-one\",\\n                \"difficulty\": \"independent\",\\n                \"expected_evidence\": \"the learner selects the statement that matches both the sign and the size of the two slopes\",\\n                \"purpose\": \"measure whether the learner reads direction and steepness from the slope number on an unfamiliar line\",\\n                \"target\": \"how the two ramps compare, given their slopes\"\\n              },\\n              \"source_question_ids\": [\\n                \"04c25319-eaa8-488c-9f5d-f9ee348fcdb7:04c25319-eaa8-488c-9f5d-f9ee348fcdb7:7f4fe57d-a07f-4d0a-92a0-185ffbefee02.i1\"\\n              ],\\n              \"sourcebook_needs\": [],\\n              \"sourcebook_refs\": [],\\n              \"stimulus_dependencies\": [],\\n              \"task_mode\": \"assessment\"\\n            }\\n          ],\\n          \"specific_purpose\": \"Check whether the learner can read the sign and size of slope numbers, including a negative and a boundary case, for a line not covered in the lesson.\",\\n          \"transition\": \"The learner has seen the rule built, its boundary shown, and the wrong beliefs tested; now the reading is measured on a new line.\"\\n        }\\n      ]\\n    },\\n    \"slot_intent_policy\": {\\n      \"check\": {\\n        \"departure_rule\": \"A permitted non-typical intent requires a specific departure_reason. Excluded intents are never legal.\",\\n        \"permitted_departures\": [\\n          \"activate-prior-knowledge\",\\n          \"apply\",\\n          \"classify\",\\n          \"compare\",\\n          \"connect-back\",\\n          \"connect-forward\",\\n          \"define\",\\n          \"demonstrate\",\\n          \"derive\",\\n          \"emphasise\",\\n          \"explain\",\\n          \"explain-cause\",\\n          \"interpret\",\\n          \"memory-aid\",\\n          \"name-parts\",\\n          \"orient\",\\n          \"practise-guided\",\\n          \"sequence\",\\n          \"show-structure\",\\n          \"summarise\",\\n          \"trace-flow\",\\n          \"warn\"\\n        ],\\n        \"typical_intents\": [\\n          \"check-understanding\",\\n          \"diagnose-misconception\",\\n          \"practise-independent\"\\n        ]\\n      },\\n      \"confront\": {\\n        \"departure_rule\": \"A permitted non-typical intent requires a specific departure_reason. Excluded intents are never legal.\",\\n        \"permitted_departure