opy the brief verbatim into the learner-facing fields.\\\\n- Honour objective, terminology, evidence / approved facts, and must_not_introduce.\\\\n- Use supplied Sourcebook entries exactly for bound values, facts, dates, names,\\\\n  labels, units, examples, and stimuli. Never silently replace a bound example.\\\\n- Do not author response tasks; those are shared TaskSpecs consumed by both paths.\\\\n- Do not reteach prior_established content; reference it briefly when useful.\\\\n- Stay coherent with neighbouring composition choices when provided.\\\\n- Do not invent placeholder rows (\\\\\"Row 1\\\\\"), generic headers (\\\\\"Item\\\\\"/\\\\\"Detail\\\\\"),\\\\n  or \\\\\"Content pending.\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\nPrimitive field contracts:\\\\n- paragraph \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"text\\\\\": string }\\\\n- heading \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"text\\\\\": string, \\\\\"level\\\\\": 1|2|3 }\\\\n- list \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"ordered\\\\\": boolean, \\\\\"items\\\\\": [string, ...] }\\\\n- table \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"headers\\\\\": [string, ...], \\\\\"rows\\\\\": [[string, ...], ...], \\\\\"caption\\\\\": string }\\\\n- callout \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"tone\\\\\": \\\\\"note\\\\\"|\\\\\"warning\\\\\"|\\\\\"tip\\\\\"|\\\\\"important\\\\\", \\\\\"title\\\\\": string, \\\\\"body\\\\\": string }\\\\n- figure \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"caption\\\\\": string, \\\\\"alt\\\\\": string }\\\\n\\\\nReturn JSON matching the assigned primitive schema only (plus kind).\\\\n\",\\n    \"knowledge\": {},\\n    \"modes\": [\\n      \"generate\"\\n    ],\\n    \"native_path\": \"document\",\\n    \"payload_schema\": {\\n      \"additionalProperties\": false,\\n      \"properties\": {\\n        \"caption\": {\\n          \"type\": \"string\"\\n        },\\n        \"headers\": {\\n          \"items\": {\\n            \"minLength\": 1,\\n            \"type\": \"string\"\\n          },\\n          \"minItems\": 1,\\n          \"type\": \"array\"\\n        },\\n        \"kind\": {\\n          \"const\": \"table\"\\n        },\\n        \"rows\": {\\n          \"items\": {\\n            \"items\": {\\n              \"type\": \"string\"\\n            },\\n            \"minItems\": 1,\\n            \"type\": \"array\"\\n          },\\n          \"minItems\": 1,\\n          \"type\": \"array\"\\n        }\\n      },\\n      \"required\": [\\n        \"kind\",\\n        \"headers\",\\n        \"rows\"\\n      ],\\n      \"type\": \"object\"\\n    },\\n    \"required_inputs\": [\\n      \"kind\",\\n      \"brief\",\\n      \"teaching_block\"\\n    ],\\n    \"validator_refs\": [\\n      \"document.writer_schema\"\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  \"inputs\": {\\n    \"allowed_facts\": [\\n      \"a primary source is a first-hand account created by someone who witnessed or took part in an event\",\\n      \"primary sources reflect the perspective and limits of their author, so accounts of the same event can differ\",\\n      \"read a short historical source and identify its author, date, and main claim\"\\n    ],\\n    \"brief\": \"Place two descriptions of the same anchor account \\\\u2014 Account A \\\\u2014 side by side. The weak description reports only the date and concludes that because the account is close to the event, it tells us what happened. The strong description uses all four features: it names what is known and unknown about the author and the author\\'s position, uses the date to state what the account could and could not observe, names the type and what that type of source typically records, and states the author\\'s purpose or the purpose the account carries implied, then draws a one-sentence limit on what this account can address. Ask the learner which description is stronger and what specifically the strong one does that the weak one does not. Do not present the weak description as merely wrong \\\\u2014 it is incomplete, and the incompleteness is the point. Do not use this block to re-explain the four features.\",\\n    \"kind\": \"table\",\\n    \"lesson_context\": {\\n      \"allowed_facts\": [\\n      