{"timestamp": "2026-09-16T15:48:12.427747+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "logger": "openai._base_client", "message": "Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'headers': {'User-Agent': 'pydantic-ai/1.107.1'}, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-23eba90d-aa44-4e4d-8eb2-97dae76130fa', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You author one already-selected educational capability. Follow the\\nsupplied AuthoringDefinition and exact output schema. Preserve block identity, objective, scope,\\nlearner action, source ownership and assessment mode. Do not select another capability or redesign\\nthe teaching plan.\\n\\nPlanning instructions are not final content. For an activity, produce a meaningful prompt, task\\ndata, justified answer relationships and useful feedback as required by the contract. Do not infer\\ncorrectness from option positions, number/word positions, example ordering or arbitrary defaults.\\nDo not copy illustrative examples as unrelated lesson content.\\n\\nTreat approved items as authoritative content. Conversion must preserve their question and answer\\nmeaning. Report incompatibility through the defined error path instead of guessing. Use supported\\nteacher-review only when the work order policy permits it.\\n\\nReturn exactly the selected output schema. Do not emit sibling schemas, markdown wrappers, internal\\nplanning metadata or extra fields. If required information is absent, use the configured\\nmissing-input mechanism; do not create placeholder content to satisfy structure.\\n\\nDo not invent or overwrite knowledge/assessment policy fields. Effective policy is code-owned.\\n\\n## KNOWLEDGE POLICY\\nKnowledge policy: use supplied facts as the primary basis when present; do not describe model knowledge as approved factual material.\\n\\n## PACKAGE CAPABILITY INSTRUCTIONS\\nYou compose ordinary lesson document STRUCTURE from an approved Teaching Plan.\\n\\nThe caller supplies `allowed_kinds_by_block`. That map is a CLOSED contract.\\nFor each teaching block, choose only from that block\\'s exact `allowed_kinds`.\\nAn empty list means the block is task-only on this path: emit no ordinary node\\nfor it. Never infer, widen, or substitute a kind outside the supplied list.\\n\\nThe only possible ordinary primitive vocabulary is:\\n  paragraph | heading | list | figure | table | callout\\n\\nReturn CHOICES only \u2014 never full content payloads.\\n\\nHard rules:\\n- Copy every `teaching_block_id` from the supplied Teaching Plan verbatim.\\n- Copy `section_id` from the supplied Teaching Plan; never invent or rename it.\\n- Choose `kind` only from that teaching block\\'s exact `allowed_kinds`.\\n- Cover every block whose `allowed_kinds` is non-empty with at least one node.\\n- Emit no node for a block whose `allowed_kinds` is empty.\\n- No component_id, template_id, page_break, ruled_lines, media blobs, or interaction nodes.\\n- Interactions are authored separately; never invent them here.\\n- One teaching block MAY become MULTIPLE ordinary nodes only when every chosen\\n  kind is independently present in that same block\\'s `allowed_kinds`.\\n- Prefer clear pedagogical order without changing the Teaching Plan meaning.\\n- Figure: emit a figure choice with role/reason only. Asset generation uses the\\n  existing figure pipeline \u2014 do not invent image bytes, asset IDs, or URLs.\\n- Keep output node ids stable and unique across the sequence.\\n- Stay within the Teaching Plan objective and must_not_introduce constraints.\\n- Do not decorate every section with tables or callouts \u2014 use them intentionally.\\n\\nDo not use global vocabulary knowledge to rescue a missing option. If the kind\\nyou would prefer is absent from a block\\'s allowed list, choose another supplied\\nkind for that block. If the list is empty, emit nothing for that block.\\n\\nReturn JSON exactly:\\n{\\n  \"nodes\": [\\n    {\\n      \"id\": \"string\",\\n      \"teaching_block_id\": \"<exact supplied block id>\",\\n      \"section_id\": \"<exact supplied section id>|null\",\\n      \"kind\": \"<one exact value from this block\\'s allowed_kinds>\",\\n      \"role\": \"string|null\",\\n      \"reason\": \"string\"\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\\n\\n\\n## AUTHORING DEFINITION\\n{\\n  \"assessment\": {},\\n  \"capability_id\": \"document-composer\",\\n  \"converter_ref\": null,\\n  \"definition_hash\": \"34068b4c8de8687d9330b5417b432ee1950a5880468246869285d821b39c9b8c\",\\n  \"instructions\": \"You compose ordinary lesson document STRUCTURE from an approved Teaching Plan.\\\\n\\\\nThe caller supplies `allowed_kinds_by_block`. That map is a CLOSED contract.\\\\nFor each teaching block, choose only from that block\\'s exact `allowed_kinds`.\\\\nAn empty list means the block is task-only on this path: emit no ordinary node\\\\nfor it. Never infer, widen, or substitute a kind outside the supplied list.\\\\n\\\\nThe only possible ordinary primitive vocabulary is:\\\\n  paragraph | heading | list | figure | table | callout\\\\n\\\\nReturn CHOICES only \\\\u2014 never full content payloads.\\\\n\\\\nHard rules:\\\\n- Copy every `teaching_block_id` from the supplied Teaching Plan verbatim.\\\\n- Copy `section_id` from the supplied Teaching Plan; never invent or rename it.\\\\n- Choose `kind` only from that teaching block\\'s exact `allowed_kinds`.\\\\n- Cover every block whose `allowed_kinds` is non-empty with at least one node.\\\\n- Emit no node for a block whose `allowed_kinds` is empty.\\\\n- No component_id, template_id, page_break, ruled_lines, media blobs, or interaction nodes.\\\\n- Interactions are authored separately; never invent them here.\\\\n- One teaching block MAY become MULTIPLE ordinary nodes only when every chosen\\\\n  kind is independently present in that same block\\'s `allowed_kinds`.\\\\n- Prefer clear pedagogical order without changing the Teaching Plan meaning.\\\\n- Figure: emit a figure choice with role/reason only. Asset generation uses the\\\\n  existing figure pipeline \\\\u2014 do not invent image bytes, asset IDs, or URLs.\\\\n- Keep output node ids stable and unique across the sequence.\\\\n- Stay within the Teaching Plan objective and must_not_introduce constraints.\\\\n- Do not decorate every section with tables or callouts \\\\u2014 use them intentionally.\\\\n\\\\nDo not use global vocabulary knowledge to rescue a missing option. If the kind\\\\nyou would prefer is absent from a block\\'s allowed list, choose another supplied\\\\nkind for that block. If the list is empty, emit nothing for that block.\\\\n\\\\nReturn JSON exactly:\\\\n{\\\\n  \\\\\"nodes\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\"id\\\\\": \\\\\"string\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\"teaching_block_id\\\\\": \\\\\"<exact supplied block id>\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\"section_id\\\\\": \\\\\"<exact supplied section id>|null\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\"kind\\\\\": \\\\\"<one exact value from this block\\'s allowed_kinds>\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\"role\\\\\": \\\\\"string|null\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\"reason\\\\\": \\\\\"string\\\\\"\\\\n    }\\\\n  ]\\\\n}\\\\n\",\\n  \"knowledge\": {},\\n  \"modes\": [\\n    \"generate\"\\n  ],\\n  \"native_path\": \"learn\",\\n  \"payload_schema\": {\\n    \"additionalProperties\": false,\\n    \"properties\": {\\n      \"nodes\": {\\n        \"items\": {\\n          \"additionalProperties\": false,\\n          \"properties\": {\\n            \"id\": {\\n              \"minLength\": 1,\\n              \"type\": \"string\"\\n            },\\n            \"kind\": {\\n              \"enum\": [\\n                \"callout\",\\n                \"figure\",\\n                \"heading\",\\n                \"list\",\\n                \"paragraph\",\\n                \"table\"\\n              ],\\n              \"type\": \"string\"\\n            },\\n            \"reason\": {\\n              \"minLength\": 1,\\n              \"type\": \"string\"\\n            },\\n            \"role\": {\\n              \"type\": \"string\"\\n            },\\n            \"section_id\": {\\n              \"type\": \"string\"\\n            },\\n            \"teaching_block_id\": {\\n              \"minLength\": 1,\\n              \"type\": \"string\"\\n            }\\n          },\\n          \"required\": [\\n            \"id\",\\n            \"teaching_block_id\",\\n            \"kind\",\\n            \"reason\"\\n          ],\\n          \"type\": \"object\"\\n        },\\n        \"minItems\": 1,\\n        \"type\": \"array\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \"required\": [\\n      \"nodes\"\\n    ],\\n    \"type\": \"object\"\\n  },\\n  \"required_inputs\": [\\n    \"teaching_plan\",\\n    \"allowed_kinds_by_block\"\\n  ],\\n  \"validator_refs\": [\\n    \"document.composer_schema\"\\n  ]\\n}\\n\\n## SCOPED REQUEST\\n{\\n  \"inputs\": {\\n    \"allowed_kinds_by_block\": {\\n      \"check-b1\": [\\n        \"callout\",\\n        \"figure\",\\n        \"heading\",\\n        \"list\",\\n        \"paragraph\",\\n        \"table\"\\n      ],\\n      \"confront-b1\": [\\n        \"callout\",\\n        \"figure\",\\n        \"heading\",\\n        \"list\",\\n        \"paragraph\",\\n        \"table\"\\n      ],\\n      \"contrast-b1\": [\\n        \"callout\",\\n        \"figure\",\\n        \"heading\",\\n        \"list\",\\n        \"paragraph\",\\n        \"table\"\\n      ],\\n      \"contrast-b2\": [\\n        \"callout\",\\n        \"figure\",\\n        \"heading\",\\n        \"list\",\\n        \"paragraph\",\\n        \"table\"\\n      ],\\n      \"explain-b1\": [\\n        \"callout\",\\n        \"figure\",\\n        \"heading\",\\n        \"list\",\\n        \"paragraph\",\\n        \"table\"\\n      ],\\n      \"explain-b2\": [\\n        \"callout\",\\n        \"figure\",\\n        \"heading\",\\n        \"list\",\\n        \"paragraph\",\\n        \"table\"\\n      ],\\n      \"explain-b3\": [\\n        \"callout\",\\n        \"figure\",\\n        \"heading\",\\n        \"list\",\\n        \"paragraph\",\\n        \"table\"\\n      ],\\n      \"orient-b1\": [\\n        \"callout\",\\n        \"figure\",\\n        \"heading\",\\n        \"list\",\\n        \"paragraph\",\\n        \"table\"\\n      ],\\n      \"orient-b2\": [\\n        \"callout\",\\n        \"figure\",\\n        \"heading\",\\n        \"list\",\\n        \"paragraph\",\\n        \"table\"\\n      ]\\n    },\\n    \"teaching_plan\": {\\n      \"arc\": \"The lesson opens on the fixed anchor \\\\u2014 two accounts of the same event written from different positions \\\\u2014 and asks students to notice that the accounts disagree before any method is named. The lesson then builds the four analytic features (author, date, type, stated-or-implied purpose) as a set of questions asked of each source, and shows how the same four features limit what each account of the pair can address. It then puts a strong and a weak description of a source side by side, surfaces and breaks the three approved wrong beliefs about anonymous authorship, dates as automatic reliability, and unwritten purposes, and closes on the approved check item for a single source.\",\\n      \"revision\": 1,\\n      \"sections\": [\\n        {\\n          \"blocks\": [\\n            {\\n              \"allowed_kinds\": [\\n                \"callout\",\\n                \"figure\",\\n                \"heading\",\\n                \"list\",\\n                \"paragraph\",\\n                \"table\"\\n              ],\\n              \"brief\": \"Present the anchor case: two accounts of the same event, written by different people in different positions. State the event, name the two accounts as Account A and Account B, and quote or paraphrase one point on which they disagree \\\\u2014 for example, what each account says happened to the same group of people at the same moment. Do not explain why they differ, do not name author, date, type, or purpose as categories yet, and do not resolve the disagreement. End on the question the lesson will answer: what do we need to find out about each account before we can say what it can address?\",\\n              \"evidence\": \"The objective requires students to analyze two given accounts, and the anchor is already a pair of accounts of the same event; opening on their disagreement gives the four analytic features a felt problem to solve rather than a checklist to memorize.\",\\n              \"id\": \"orient-b1\",\\n              \"intent\": \"orient\",\\n              \"learner_action\": null\\n            },\\n            {\\n              \"allowed_kinds\": [\\n                \"callout\",\\n                \"figure\",\\n                \"heading\",\\n                \"list\",\\n                \"paragraph\",\\n                \"table\"\\n              ],\\n              \"brief\": \"Prompt recall of two prior ideas by ID: that a primary source is a first-hand account created by someone who witnessed or took part in the event (prior-1), and that primary sources reflect the perspective and limits of their author, so accounts of the same event can differ (prior-2). Ask the learner to say, in one or two sentences, why prior-2 makes the anchor\\'s disagreement expected rather than surprising. Do not yet introduce author, date, type, or purpose as the features to inspect.\",\\n              \"evidence\": \"prior-2 is the exact prerequisite that makes the anchor\\'s disagreement intelligible, and the orient slot exists to give the learner a reason to care before the method is named; recalling it here means the explain slot does not have to spend its space on definition.\",\\n              \"id\": \"orient-b2\",\\n              \"intent\": \"activate-prior-knowledge\",\\n              \"learner_action\": null\\n            }\\n          ],\\n          \"slot_id\": \"orient\",\\n          \"specific_purpose\": \"Open attention on the anchor\\'s two accounts and give the learner a felt problem: the pair disagrees, and the learner must be able to say what makes the disagreement usable rather than disqualifying.\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"blocks\": [\\n            {\\n              \"allowed_kinds\": [\\n                \"callout\",\\n                \"figure\",\\n                \"heading\",\\n                \"list\",\\n                \"paragraph\",\\n                \"table\"\\n              ],\\n              \"brief\": \"Name the four features the learner must identify in any primary source: author, date, type, and stated or implied purpose. For each feature, state the question it answers about the source \\\\u2014 who made it and from what position; when it was made relative to the event; what kind of thing it is; what it was made to do. Apply each question to the anchor\\'s Account A and Account B in turn, naming what is known and what is not for each. Do not yet state how a feature can be implied rather than stated, and do not yet say what each feature rules in or out for the account.\",\\n              \"evidence\": \"The objective names exactly four things to identify \\\\u2014 author, date, type, and stated or implied purpose \\\\u2014 so the explain slot must first establish them as the parts of the method before any of them can be shown shaping what a source can address.\",\\n              \"id\": \"explain-b1\",\\n              \"intent\": \"name-parts\",\\n              \"learner_action\": null\\n            },\\n            {\\n              \"allowed_kinds\": [\\n                \"callout\",\\n                \"figure\",\\n                \"heading\",\\n                \"list\",\\n                \"paragraph\",\\n                \"table\"\\n              ],\\n              \"brief\": \"Show the mechanism linking each of the four features to what the account can address, using the anchor\\'s two accounts. Take author first: a participant who saw the event from one side can address what that side experienced but not what the other side experienced. Take date next: a source made close to the event can address immediate conditions and reactions but not later consequences; a source made later can address memory and retrospective meaning but not unobserved detail. Take type: a private letter can address personal reaction, a public notice can address intended message, an official record can address what an institution tracked. Take purpose: a source made to persuade can address what its maker wanted readers to do, and can address its own claims only with suspicion. Use Account A and Account B concretely at least twice. Do not state that a later date is less reliable, and do not state that an unnamed author means no author \\\\u2014 those beliefs are confronted later.\",\\n              \"evidence\": \"The objective asks students to describe how each feature SHAPES what the source can address, which is a causal claim from feature to limit, and explain-cause is the intent whose choose_when names a mechanism between cause and result that the learner must be able to state.\",\\n              \"id\": \"explain-b2\",\\n              \"intent\": \"explain-cause\",\\n              \"learner_action\": null\\n            },\\n            {\\n              \"allowed_kinds\": [\\n                \"callout\",\\n                \"figure\",\\n                \"heading\",\\n                \"list\",\\n                \"paragraph\",\\n                \"table\"\\n              ],\\n              \"brief\": \"Draw the four features together into a single usable model: author, date, type, and stated or implied purpose are not four separate verdicts about a source but four answers that, taken together, describe the window through which the source sees the event \\\\u2014 what it could observe, when it could observe it, in what form it recorded it, and for whom. State explicitly that \\'implied purpose\\' means a purpose the source carries without writing it down, which can be identified from what the source does \\\\u2014 what it asks readers to do, what it leaves out, what it repeats. Apply the combined model to Account A and Account B in one sentence each, naming the window each gives. Do not re-explain features one by one; that belongs to the earlier block.\",\\n              \"evidence\": \"The objective requires describing how the four features together shape what a source can address, so after the parts and the per-feature mechanism the learner needs one integrated model rather than four disconnected rules; the implied-purpose clarification also belongs here rather than in the confront section.\",\\n              \"id\": \"explain-b3\",\\n              \"intent\": \"explain\",\\n              \"learner_action\": null\\n            }\\n          ],\\n          \"slot_id\": \"explain\",\\n          \"specific_purpose\": \"Build the analytic method itself: the four features \\\\u2014 author, date, type, and stated or implied purpose \\\\u2014 as questions to ask of any primary source, each grounded in the anchor\\'s two accounts so the connection between feature and what the account can address is visible.\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"blocks\": [\\n            {\\n              \"allowed_kinds\": [\\n                \"callout\",\\n                \"figure\",\\n                \"heading\",\\n                \"list\",\\n                \"paragraph\",\\n                \"table\"\\n              ],\\n              \"brief\": \"Place two descriptions of the same anchor account \\\\u2014 Account A \\\\u2014 side by side. The weak description reports only the date and concludes that because the account is close to the event, it tells us what happened. The strong description uses all four features: it names what is known and unknown about the author and the author\\'s position, uses the date to state what the account could and could not observe, names the type and what that type of source typically records, and states the author\\'s purpose or the purpose the account carries implied, then draws a one-sentence limit on what this account can address. Ask the learner which description is stronger and what specifically the strong one does that the weak one does not. Do not present the weak description as merely wrong \\\\u2014 it is incomplete, and the incompleteness is the point. Do not use this block to re-explain the four features.\",\\n              \"evidence\": \"The contrast slot requires a case and a near-miss side by side, and the near-miss here is a date-only description, which is the visible form of M2; placing them together lets the learner see the boundary of a complete analysis rather than only hear the rule.\",\\n              \"id\": \"contrast-b1\",\\n              \"intent\": \"compare\",\\n              \"learner_action\": null\\n            },\\n            {\\n              \"allowed_kinds\": [\\n                \"callout\",\\n                \"figure\",\\n                \"heading\",\\n                \"list\",\\n                \"paragraph\",\\n                \"table\"\\n              ],\\n              \"brief\": \"Give the learner four short description fragments about the anchor\\'s two accounts and have them sort each into \\'uses the features to say what the source can address\\' or \\'does not.\\' At least one fragment must use the date alone as a verdict, at least one must treat an unnamed author as no author, and at least one must treat an unwritten purpose as no purpose. After the sort, state the rule for the boundary: a description belongs on the strong side only if it states a limit that follows from at least two of the four features, not from a single feature used as a verdict. Do not name M1, M2, or M3 as misconceptions here \\\\u2014 the confrontation section does that; this block only establishes the sorting rule.\",\\n              \"evidence\": \"The contrast slot asks for the boundary of the idea, not just the idea, and a sorting task forces the learner to apply the two-feature rule to fragments drawn from the same anchor pair rather than only reading a comparison.\",\\n              \"id\": \"contrast-b2\",\\n              \"intent\": \"classify\",\\n              \"learner_action\": null\\n            }\\n          ],\\n          \"slot_id\": \"contrast\",\\n          \"specific_purpose\": \"Put a strong and a weak description of one of the anchor\\'s accounts side by side so the learner sees the boundary of a complete analysis \\\\u2014 what counts as having used the features and what does not.\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"blocks\": [\\n            {\\n              \"allowed_kinds\": [\\n                \"callout\",\\n                \"figure\",\\n                \"heading\",\\n                \"list\",\\n                \"paragraph\",\\n                \"table\"\\n              ],\\n              \"brief\": \"Take the three approved wrong beliefs in turn and fail each on the anchor. For M1, \\'if a source does not say who wrote it, it has no author and can be treated as neutral\\': show why it feels right \\\\u2014 an unnamed source looks impartial \\\\u2014 then fail it: an unidentified author is an unknown position, not a neutral one, and the anchor\\'s account that does not name its writer still shows a point of view in what it includes and omits. For M2, \\'a source closer in time is automatically more reliable and the date alone settles what the source can be used for\\': show why it feels right \\\\u2014 proximity to the event sounds like better access \\\\u2014 then fail it: a date close to the event limits the source to immediate observation and cannot speak to later consequences, and the anchor\\'s later account can address remembered experience that the earlier one cannot. For M3, \\'purpose counts only if the source states it, and no written purpose means it can be read as a plain record\\': show why it feels right \\\\u2014 an unstated purpose is not on the page \\\\u2014 then fail it: purpose can be identified from what the source asks readers to do and what it suppresses, and one of the anchor\\'s accounts carries a purpose it never names. End by restating the correct position: an unnamed author, a given date, and an unstated purpose each still shape what the source can address \\\\u2014 they do not remove the need to ask the four questions.\",\\n              \"evidence\": \"All three misconceptions are held beliefs that produce confidently wrong analyses, and the confront slot\\'s purpose explicitly requires naming the wrong belief, showing why it feels right, and showing it failing on a case \\\\u2014 so the block must test each belief against the anchor\\'s accounts rather than merely restate the correct rule.\",\\n              \"id\": \"confront-b1\",\\n              \"intent\": \"diagnose-misconception\",\\n              \"learner_action\": null\\n            }\\n          ],\\n          \"slot_id\": \"confront\",\\n          \"specific_purpose\": \"Name the three approved wrong beliefs, show why each feels right, and show each failing on the anchor\\'s two accounts so the learner stops holding them as general rules.\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"blocks\": [\\n            {\\n              \"allowed_kinds\": [\\n                \"callout\",\\n                \"figure\",\\n                \"heading\",\\n                \"list\",\\n                \"paragraph\",\\n                \"table\"\\n              ],\\n              \"brief\": \"Administer the approved check item as written. The learner reads the item\\'s source case, answers it, and the teacher records the response for the diagnostic. No additional explanation, scaffolding, or revision of the method belongs in this block; it is the measurement step and is drawn from the approved item only.\",\\n              \"evidence\": \"The check slot\\'s purpose is the shared diagnostic, and the approved item i5 asks which analysis best follows the objective on an unnamed-photographer photograph \\\\u2014 the closest match to a source whose author, date, type, and purpose must all be inferred.\",\\n              \"id\": \"check-b1\",\\n              \"intent\": \"check-understanding\",\\n              \"learner_action\": {\\n                \"action\": \"select-one\",\\n                \"purpose\": \"measure whether the learner can identify author, date, type, and purpose on an unfamiliar source and describe what that source can address\",\\n                \"target\": \"the approved check item on an unnamed-photographer photograph\"\\n              }\\n            }\\n          ],\\n          \"slot_id\": \"check\",\\n          \"specific_purpose\": \"Run the shared diagnostic exactly as approved: one item on one source, checking whether the learner can identify the features and describe what the source can address.\"\\n        }\\n      ],\\n      \"teaching_plan_id\": \"75fdff8d-7118-4ff8-b82a-7e652d9611a2\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \"mode\": \"generate\",\\n  \"resolved_policy\": {\\n    \"allow_heuristic_composition_fallback\": true,\\n    \"closed_per_block_allowlists\": true\\n  },\\n  \"scoped_request\": {\\n    \"allow_heuristic_fallback\": true,\\n    \"allowed_kinds_by_block\": {\\n      \"check-b1\": [\\n        \"callout\",\\n        \"figure\",\\n        \"heading\",\\n        \"list\",\\n        \"paragraph\",\\n        \"table\"\\n      ],\\n      \"confront-b1\": [\\n        \"callout\",\\n        \"figure\",\\n        \"heading\",\\n        \"list\",\\n        \"paragraph\",\\n        \"table\"\\n      ],\\n      \"contrast-b1\": [\\n        \"callout\",\\n        \"figure\",\\n        \"heading\",\\n        \"list\",\\n        \"paragraph\",\\n        \"table\"\\n      ],\\n      \"contrast-b2\": [\\n        \"callout\",\\n        \"figure\",\\n        \"heading\",\\n        \"list\",\\n        \"paragraph\",\\n        \"table\"\\n      ],\\n      \"explain-b1\": [\\n        \"callout\",\\n        \"figure\",\\n        \"heading\",\\n        \"list\",\\n        \"paragraph\",\\n        \"table\