lness for supporting a given claim using relevant criteria (authorship, position, proximity, purpose, corroboration)\",\\n      \"justify which claims each source can support by referring to the source\\'s own evidence and limitations\"\\n    ],\\n    \"brief\": \"Present the anchor case: two accounts of the same event, written by different people in different positions. State the event, name the two accounts as Account A and Account B, and quote or paraphrase one point on which they disagree \\\\u2014 for example, what each account says happened to the same group of people at the same moment. Do not explain why they differ, do not name author, date, type, or purpose as categories yet, and do not resolve the disagreement. End on the question the lesson will answer: what do we need to find out about each account before we can say what it can address?\",\\n    \"kind\": \"callout\",\\n    \"lesson_context\": {\\n      \"input_availability\": \"supplied_statements\",\\n      \"objective\": \"Identify the author, date, type, and stated or implied purpose of two given primary-source accounts and describe how each of these features shapes what the source can address\",\\n      \"sourcebook_entries\": [],\\n      \"subject\": \"History\",\\n      \"teaching_plan_revision\": 1,\\n      \"title\": \"The lesson opens on the fixed anchor \\\\u2014 two accounts of the same event written from different positions \\\\u2014 and asks students to notice that the accounts disagree before any method is named. The lesson then builds the four analytic features (author, date, type, stated-or-implied purpose) as a set of questions asked of each source, and shows how the same four features limit what each account of the pair can address. It then puts a strong and a weak description of a source side by side, surfaces and breaks the three approved wrong beliefs about anonymous authorship, dates as automatic reliability, and unwritten purposes, and closes on the approved check item for a single source.\"\\n    },\\n    \"neighbours\": [\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"heading\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Gives the anchor case a clear entry point so the learner knows the lesson begins with two disagreeing accounts rather than with a method.\",\\n        \"role\": \"anchor-case-title\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"orient-b1\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Presents the event and the two accounts as Account A and Account B, states the point on which they disagree, and ends on the guiding question without resolving the disagreement or naming the four features.\",\\n        \"role\": \"anchor-case-presentation\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"orient-b1\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"list\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Recalls prior-1 and prior-2 by ID as two short statements so the learner can connect the anchor\\'s disagreement to the approved prior knowledge before the method is named.\",\\n        \"role\": \"prior-idea-recall\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"orient-b2\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Asks the learner to explain in one or two sentences why prior-2 makes the anchor\\'s disagreement expected, activating prior knowledge without introducing author, date, type, or purpose as features yet.\",\\n        \"role\": \"prior-idea-application-prompt\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"orient-b2\"\\n      }\\n    ],\\n    \"reason\": \"Foregrounds the question the lesson will answer so the disagreement functions as a problem rather than a disqualification.\",\\n    \"role\": \"guiding-question\",\\n    \"sourcebook_entries\": [],\\n    \"teaching_block\": {\\n      \"brief\": \"Present the anchor case: two accounts of the same event, written by different people in different positions. State the event, name the two accounts as Account A and Account B, and quote or paraphrase one point on which they disagree \\\\u2014 for example,