{"timestamp": "2026-09-16T15:49:21.079797+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "logger": "openai._base_client", "message": "Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'headers': {'User-Agent': 'pydantic-ai/1.107.1'}, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-c38eff4c-a518-4349-bcc6-3beb1b1b7083', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You author one already-selected educational capability. Follow the\\nsupplied AuthoringDefinition and exact output schema. Preserve block identity, objective, scope,\\nlearner action, source ownership and assessment mode. Do not select another capability or redesign\\nthe teaching plan.\\n\\nPlanning instructions are not final content. For an activity, produce a meaningful prompt, task\\ndata, justified answer relationships and useful feedback as required by the contract. Do not infer\\ncorrectness from option positions, number/word positions, example ordering or arbitrary defaults.\\nDo not copy illustrative examples as unrelated lesson content.\\n\\nTreat approved items as authoritative content. Conversion must preserve their question and answer\\nmeaning. Report incompatibility through the defined error path instead of guessing. Use supported\\nteacher-review only when the work order policy permits it.\\n\\nReturn exactly the selected output schema. Do not emit sibling schemas, markdown wrappers, internal\\nplanning metadata or extra fields. If required information is absent, use the configured\\nmissing-input mechanism; do not create placeholder content to satisfy structure.\\n\\nDo not invent or overwrite knowledge/assessment policy fields. Effective policy is code-owned.\\n\\n## KNOWLEDGE POLICY\\nKnowledge policy: use supplied facts as the primary basis when present; do not describe model knowledge as approved factual material.\\n\\n## PACKAGE CAPABILITY INSTRUCTIONS\\nYou write LEARNER-FACING content for ONE ordinary document primitive.\\n\\nAllowed kinds: paragraph, heading, list, table, callout, figure.\\n(Figure: write caption + alt only. Asset generation uses the figure pipeline.)\\n\\nHard rules:\\n- Fill ONLY fields for the assigned kind. No unrelated fields.\\n- No component_id, template_id, page_break, ruled_lines, or media objects.\\n- Do not author interactions; those use the interaction writer.\\n- The Teaching Plan brief is a WRITER INSTRUCTION, not student-facing text.\\n  Never copy the brief verbatim into the learner-facing fields.\\n- Honour objective, terminology, evidence / approved facts, and must_not_introduce.\\n- Use supplied Sourcebook entries exactly for bound values, facts, dates, names,\\n  labels, units, examples, and stimuli. Never silently replace a bound example.\\n- Do not author response tasks; those are shared TaskSpecs consumed by both paths.\\n- Do not reteach prior_established content; reference it briefly when useful.\\n- Stay coherent with neighbouring composition choices when provided.\\n- Do not invent placeholder rows (\"Row 1\"), generic headers (\"Item\"/\"Detail\"),\\n  or \"Content pending.\"\\n\\nPrimitive field contracts:\\n- paragraph \u2192 { \"text\": string }\\n- heading \u2192 { \"text\": string, \"level\": 1|2|3 }\\n- list \u2192 { \"ordered\": boolean, \"items\": [string, ...] }\\n- table \u2192 { \"headers\": [string, ...], \"rows\": [[string, ...], ...], \"caption\": string }\\n- callout \u2192 { \"tone\": \"note\"|\"warning\"|\"tip\"|\"important\", \"title\": string, \"body\": string }\\n- figure \u2192 { \"caption\": string, \"alt\": string }\\n\\nReturn JSON matching the assigned primitive schema only (plus kind).\\n\\n\\n## AUTHORING DEFINITION\\n{\\n  \"assessment\": {},\\n  \"capability_id\": \"document-writer:callout\",\\n  \"converter_ref\": null,\\n  \"definition_hash\": \"22fefe3ecb3c07b4dd776eba11349b83db5548bf3b00e3b39d96e9d0f85fda3b:callout\",\\n  \"instructions\": \"You write LEARNER-FACING content for ONE ordinary document primitive.\\\\n\\\\nAllowed kinds: paragraph, heading, list, table, callout, figure.\\\\n(Figure: write caption + alt only. Asset generation uses the figure pipeline.)\\\\n\\\\nHard rules:\\\\n- Fill ONLY fields for the assigned kind. No unrelated fields.\\\\n- No component_id, template_id, page_break, ruled_lines, or media objects.\\\\n- Do not author interactions; those use the interaction writer.\\\\n- The Teaching Plan brief is a WRITER INSTRUCTION, not student-facing text.\\\\n  Never copy the brief verbatim into the learner-facing fields.\\\\n- Honour objective, terminology, evidence / approved facts, and must_not_introduce.\\\\n- Use supplied Sourcebook entries exactly for bound values, facts, dates, names,\\\\n  labels, units, examples, and stimuli. Never silently replace a bound example.\\\\n- Do not author response tasks; those are shared TaskSpecs consumed by both paths.\\\\n- Do not reteach prior_established content; reference it briefly when useful.\\\\n- Stay coherent with neighbouring composition choices when provided.\\\\n- Do not invent placeholder rows (\\\\\"Row 1\\\\\"), generic headers (\\\\\"Item\\\\\"/\\\\\"Detail\\\\\"),\\\\n  or \\\\\"Content pending.\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\nPrimitive field contracts:\\\\n- paragraph \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"text\\\\\": string }\\\\n- heading \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"text\\\\\": string, \\\\\"level\\\\\": 1|2|3 }\\\\n- list \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"ordered\\\\\": boolean, \\\\\"items\\\\\": [string, ...] }\\\\n- table \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"headers\\\\\": [string, ...], \\\\\"rows\\\\\": [[string, ...], ...], \\\\\"caption\\\\\": string }\\\\n- callout \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"tone\\\\\": \\\\\"note\\\\\"|\\\\\"warning\\\\\"|\\\\\"tip\\\\\"|\\\\\"important\\\\\", \\\\\"title\\\\\": string, \\\\\"body\\\\\": string }\\\\n- figure \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"caption\\\\\": string, \\\\\"alt\\\\\": string }\\\\n\\\\nReturn JSON matching the assigned primitive schema only (plus kind).\\\\n\",\\n  \"knowledge\": {},\\n  \"modes\": [\\n    \"generate\"\\n  ],\\n  \"native_path\": \"document\",\\n  \"payload_schema\": {\\n    \"additionalProperties\": false,\\n    \"properties\": {\\n      \"body\": {\\n        \"minLength\": 1,\\n        \"type\": \"string\"\\n      },\\n      \"kind\": {\\n        \"const\": \"callout\"\\n      },\\n      \"title\": {\\n        \"type\": \"string\"\\n      },\\n      \"tone\": {\\n        \"enum\": [\\n          \"note\",\\n          \"warning\",\\n          \"tip\",\\n          \"important\"\\n        ],\\n        \"type\": \"string\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \"required\": [\\n      \"kind\",\\n      \"tone\",\\n      \"body\"\\n    ],\\n    \"type\": \"object\"\\n  },\\n  \"required_inputs\": [\\n    \"kind\",\\n    \"brief\",\\n    \"teaching_block\"\\n  ],\\n  \"validator_refs\": [\\n    \"document.writer_schema\"\\n  ]\\n}\\n\\n## SCOPED REQUEST\\n{\\n  \"inputs\": {\\n    \"allowed_facts\": [\\n      \"a primary source is a first-hand account created by someone who witnessed or took part in an event\",\\n      \"primary sources reflect the perspective and limits of their author, so accounts of the same event can differ\",\\n      \"read a short historical source and identify its author, date, and main claim\",\\n      \"For each source, name its author, or state that the author is unknown, and note the author\\'s role or position relative to the event.\",\\n      \"For each source, state its date and whether it is contemporary with the event or written later.\",\\n      \"For each source, describe its stated or inferred purpose (e.g., to report, persuade, record, commemorate).\",\\n      \"Explain how an author\\'s role, date, or purpose narrows or expands the kinds of claims the source can speak to.\",\\n      \"identify the author, date, and stated purpose of each primary-source account and explain how that context shapes what the source can say\",\\n      \"identify the specific claims each primary-source account makes about the same event\",\\n      \"evaluate each source\\'s usefulness for supporting a given claim using relevant criteria (authorship, position, proximity, purpose, corroboration)\",\\n      \"justify which claims each source can support by referring to the source\\'s own evidence and limitations\"\\n    ],\\n    \"brief\": \"Take the three approved wrong beliefs in turn and fail each on the anchor. For M1, \\'if a source does not say who wrote it, it has no author and can be treated as neutral\\': show why it feels right \\\\u2014 an unnamed source looks impartial \\\\u2014 then fail it: an unidentified author is an unknown position, not a neutral one, and the anchor\\'s account that does not name its writer still shows a point of view in what it includes and omits. For M2, \\'a source closer in time is automatically more reliable and the date alone settles what the source can be used for\\': show why it feels right \\\\u2014 proximity to the event sounds like better access \\\\u2014 then fail it: a date close to the event limits the source to immediate observation and cannot speak to later consequences, and the anchor\\'s later account can address remembered experience that the earlier one cannot. For M3, \\'purpose counts only if the source states it, and no written purpose means it can be read as a plain record\\': show why it feels right \\\\u2014 an unstated purpose is not on the page \\\\u2014 then fail it: purpose can be identified from what the source asks readers to do and what it suppresses, and one of the anchor\\'s accounts carries a purpose it never names. End by restating the correct position: an unnamed author, a given date, and an unstated purpose each still shape what the source can address \\\\u2014 they do not remove the need to ask the four questions.\",\\n    \"kind\": \"callout\",\\n    \"lesson_context\": {\\n      \"input_availability\": \"supplied_statements\",\\n      \"objective\": \"Identify the author, date, type, and stated or implied purpose of two given primary-source accounts and describe how each of these features shapes what the source can address\",\\n      \"sourcebook_entries\": [],\\n      \"subject\": \"History\",\\n      \"teaching_plan_revision\": 1,\\n      \"title\": \"The lesson opens on the fixed anchor \\\\u2014 two accounts of the same event written from different positions \\\\u2014 and asks students to notice that the accounts disagree before any method is named. The lesson then builds the four analytic features (author, date, type, stated-or-implied purpose) as a set of questions asked of each source, and shows how the same four features limit what each account of the pair can address. It then puts a strong and a weak description of a source side by side, surfaces and breaks the three approved wrong beliefs about anonymous authorship, dates as automatic reliability, and unwritten purposes, and closes on the approved check item for a single source.\"\\n    },\\n    \"neighbours\": [\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"heading\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Introduces the confrontation with the three approved wrong beliefs so the diagnoses are clearly separated from the earlier method building.\",\\n        \"role\": \"misconception-title\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"confront-b1\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"list\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Takes each wrong belief in turn and gives why it feels right and how it fails on the anchor, a parallel three-part structure that a list presents compactly.\",\\n        \"role\": \"misconception-diagnoses\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"confront-b1\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Administers the approved check item as written, directing the learner to read the source case and answer without adding scaffolding or revising the method.\",\\n        \"role\": \"check-administration\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"check-b1\"\\n      }\\n    ],\\n    \"reason\": \"Restates the correct position that an unnamed author, a given date, and an unstated purpose each still shape what the source can address and do not remove the need to ask the four questions.\",\\n    \"role\": \"correct-position\",\\n    \"sourcebook_entries\": [],\\n    \"teaching_block\": {\\n      \"brief\": \"Take the three approved wrong beliefs in turn and fail each on the anchor. For M1, \\'if a source does not say who wrote it, it has no author and can be treated as neutral\\': show why it feels right \\\\u2014 an unnamed source looks impartial \\\\u2014 then fail it: an unidentified author is an unknown position, not a neutral one, and the anchor\\'s account that does not name its writer still shows a point of view in what it includes and omits. For M2, \\'a source closer in time is automatically more reliable and the date alone settles what the source can be used for\\': show why it feels right \\\\u2014 proximity to the event sounds like better access \\\\u2014 then fail it: a date close to the event limits the source to immediate observation and cannot speak to later consequences, and the anchor\\'s later account can address remembered experience that the earlier one cannot. For M3, \\'purpose counts only if the source states it, and no written purpose means it can be read as a plain record\\': show why it feels right \\\\u2014 an unstated purpose is not on the page \\\\u2014 then fail it: purpose can be identified from what the source asks readers to do and what it suppresses, and one of the anchor\\'s accounts carries a purpose it never names. End by restating the correct position: an unnamed author, a given date, and an unstated purpose each still shape what the source can address \\\\u2014 they do not remove the need to ask the four questions.\",\\n      \"evidence\": \"All three misconceptions are held beliefs that produce confidently wrong analyses, and the confront slot\\'s purpose explicitly requires naming the wrong belief, showing why it feels right, and showing it failing on a case \\\\u2014 so the block must test each belief against the anchor\\'s accounts rather than merely restate the correct rule.\",\\n      \"id\": \"confront-b1\",\\n      \"intent\": \"diagnose-misconception\",\\n      \"sourcebook_refs\": []\\n    },\\n    \"terminology\": [\\n      \"primary source\",\\n      \"claim\",\\n      \"evidence\",\\n      \"author\",\\n      \"date\",\\n      \"purpose\",\\n      \"perspective\",\\n      \"bias\",\\n      \"corroboration\",\\n      \"limitation\",\\n      \"support\"\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  \"mode\": \"generate\",\\n  \"scoped_request\": {\\n    \"allowed_facts\": [\\n      \"a primary source is a first-hand account created by someone who witnessed or took part in an event\",\\n      \"primary sources reflect the perspective and limits of their author, so accounts of the same event can differ\",\\n      \"read a short historical source and identify its author, date, and main claim\",\\n      \"For each source, name its author, or state that the author is unknown, and note the author\\'s role or position relative to the event.\",\\n      \"For each source, state its date and whether it is contemporary with the event or written later.\",\\n      \"For each source, describe its stated or inferred purpose (e.g., to report, persuade, record, commemorate).\",\\n      \"Explain how an author\\'s role, date, or purpose narrows or expands the kinds of claims the source can speak to.\",\\n      \"identify the author, date, and stated purpose of each primary-source account and explain how that context shapes what the source can say\",\\n      \"identify the specific claims each primary-source account makes about the same event\",\\n      \"evaluate each source\\'s usefulness for supporting a given claim using relevant criteria (authorship, position, proximity, purpose, corroboration)\",\\n      \"justify which claims each source can support by referring to the source\\'s own evidence and limitations\"\\n    ],\\n    \"brief\": \"Take the three approved wrong beliefs in