dentify the author, date, type, and stated or implied purpose of two given primary-source accounts and describe how each of these features shapes what the source can address\",\\n    \"reason\": \"Restates the correct position that an unnamed author, a given date, and an unstated purpose each still shape what the source can address and do not remove the need to ask the four questions.\",\\n    \"role\": \"correct-position\",\\n    \"sourcebook_entries\": [],\\n    \"stage\": \"document_writing\",\\n    \"teaching_block\": {\\n      \"brief\": \"Take the three approved wrong beliefs in turn and fail each on the anchor. For M1, \\'if a source does not say who wrote it, it has no author and can be treated as neutral\\': show why it feels right \\\\u2014 an unnamed source looks impartial \\\\u2014 then fail it: an unidentified author is an unknown position, not a neutral one, and the anchor\\'s account that does not name its writer still shows a point of view in what it includes and omits. For M2, \\'a source closer in time is automatically more reliable and the date alone settles what the source can be used for\\': show why it feels right \\\\u2014 proximity to the event sounds like better access \\\\u2014 then fail it: a date close to the event limits the source to immediate observation and cannot speak to later consequences, and the anchor\\'s later account can address remembered experience that the earlier one cannot. For M3, \\'purpose counts only if the source states it, and no written purpose means it can be read as a plain record\\': show why it feels right \\\\u2014 an unstated purpose is not on the page \\\\u2014 then fail it: purpose can be identified from what the source asks readers to do and what it suppresses, and one of the anchor\\'s accounts carries a purpose it never names. End by restating the correct position: an unnamed author, a given date, and an unstated purpose each still shape what the source can address \\\\u2014 they do not remove the need t