{"timestamp": "2026-09-16T16:41:31.376460+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "logger": "openai._base_client", "message": "Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'headers': {'User-Agent': 'pydantic-ai/1.107.1'}, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-c33d87a2-098d-46dc-9612-f1ed25001e3b', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You author one already-selected educational capability. Follow the\\nsupplied AuthoringDefinition and exact output schema. Preserve block identity, objective, scope,\\nlearner action, source ownership and assessment mode. Do not select another capability or redesign\\nthe teaching plan.\\n\\nPlanning instructions are not final content. For an activity, produce a meaningful prompt, task\\ndata, justified answer relationships and useful feedback as required by the contract. Do not infer\\ncorrectness from option positions, number/word positions, example ordering or arbitrary defaults.\\nDo not copy illustrative examples as unrelated lesson content.\\n\\nTreat approved items as authoritative content. Conversion must preserve their question and answer\\nmeaning. Report incompatibility through the defined error path instead of guessing. Use supported\\nteacher-review only when the work order policy permits it.\\n\\nReturn exactly the selected output schema. Do not emit sibling schemas, markdown wrappers, internal\\nplanning metadata or extra fields. If required information is absent, use the configured\\nmissing-input mechanism; do not create placeholder content to satisfy structure.\\n\\nDo not invent or overwrite knowledge/assessment policy fields. Effective policy is code-owned.\\n\\n## KNOWLEDGE POLICY\\nKnowledge policy: use supplied facts as the primary basis when present; do not describe model knowledge as approved factual material.\\n\\n## PACKAGE CAPABILITY INSTRUCTIONS\\nYou compose ordinary lesson document STRUCTURE from an approved Teaching Plan.\\n\\nThe caller supplies `allowed_kinds_by_block`. That map is a CLOSED contract.\\nFor each teaching block, choose only from that block\\'s exact `allowed_kinds`.\\nAn empty list means the block is task-only on this path: emit no ordinary node\\nfor it. Never infer, widen, or substitute a kind outside the supplied list.\\n\\nThe only possible ordinary primitive vocabulary is:\\n  paragraph | heading | list | figure | table | callout\\n\\nReturn CHOICES only \u2014 never full content payloads.\\n\\nHard rules:\\n- Copy every `teaching_block_id` from the supplied Teaching Plan verbatim.\\n- Copy `section_id` from the supplied Teaching Plan; never invent or rename it.\\n- Choose `kind` only from that teaching block\\'s exact `allowed_kinds`.\\n- Cover every block whose `allowed_kinds` is non-empty with at least one node.\\n- Emit no node for a block whose `allowed_kinds` is empty.\\n- No component_id, template_id, page_break, ruled_lines, media blobs, or interaction nodes.\\n- Interactions are authored separately; never invent them here.\\n- One teaching block MAY become MULTIPLE ordinary nodes only when every chosen\\n  kind is independently present in that same block\\'s `allowed_kinds`.\\n- Prefer clear pedagogical order without changing the Teaching Plan meaning.\\n- Figure: emit a figure choice with role/reason only. Asset generation uses the\\n  existing figure pipeline \u2014 do not invent image bytes, asset IDs, or URLs.\\n- Keep output node ids stable and unique across the sequence.\\n- Stay within the Teaching Plan objective and must_not_introduce constraints.\\n- Do not decorate every section with tables or callouts \u2014 use them intentionally.\\n\\nDo not use global vocabulary knowledge to rescue a missing option. If the kind\\nyou would prefer is absent from a block\\'s allowed list, choose another supplied\\nkind for that block. If the list is empty, emit nothing for that block.\\n\\nReturn JSON exactly:\\n{\\n  \"nodes\": [\\n    {\\n      \"id\": \"string\",\\n      \"teaching_block_id\": \"<exact supplied block id>\",\\n      \"section_id\": \"<exact supplied section id>|null\",\\n      \"kind\": \"<one exact value from this block\\'s allowed_kinds>\",\\n      \"role\": \"string|null\",\\n      \"reason\": \"string\"\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\\n\\n\\n## AUTHORING DEFINITION\\n{\\n  \"assessment\": {},\\n  \"capability_id\": \"document-composer\",\\n  \"converter_ref\": null,\\n  \"definition_hash\": \"34068b4c8de8687d9330b5417b432ee1950a5880468246869285d821b39c9b8c\",\\n  \"instructions\": \"You compose ordinary lesson document STRUCTURE from an approved Teaching Plan.\\\\n\\\\nThe caller supplies `allowed_kinds_by_block`. That map is a CLOSED contract.\\\\nFor each teaching block, choose only from that block\\'s exact `allowed_kinds`.\\\\nAn empty list means the block is task-only on this path: emit no ordinary node\\\\nfor it. Never infer, widen, or substitute a kind outside the supplied list.\\\\n\\\\nThe only possible ordinary primitive vocabulary is:\\\\n  paragraph | heading | list | figure | table | callout\\\\n\\\\nReturn CHOICES only \\\\u2014 never full content payloads.\\\\n\\\\nHard rules:\\\\n- Copy every `teaching_block_id` from the supplied Teaching Plan verbatim.\\\\n- Copy `section_id` from the supplied Teaching Plan; never invent or rename it.\\\\n- Choose `kind` only from that teaching block\\'s exact `allowed_kinds`.\\\\n- Cover every block whose `allowed_kinds` is non-empty with at least one node.\\\\n- Emit no node for a block whose `allowed_kinds` is empty.\\\\n- No component_id, template_id, page_break, ruled_lines, media blobs, or interaction nodes.\\\\n- Interactions are authored separately; never invent them here.\\\\n- One teaching block MAY become MULTIPLE ordinary nodes only when every chosen\\\\n  kind is independently present in that same block\\'s `allowed_kinds`.\\\\n- Prefer clear pedagogical order without changing the Teaching Plan meaning.\\\\n- Figure: emit a figure choice with role/reason only. Asset generation uses the\\\\n  existing figure pipeline \\\\u2014 do not invent image bytes, asset IDs, or URLs.\\\\n- Keep output node ids stable and unique across the sequence.\\\\n- Stay within the Teaching Plan objective and must_not_introduce constraints.\\\\n- Do not decorate every section with tables or callouts \\\\u2014 use them intentionally.\\\\n\\\\nDo not use global vocabulary knowledge to rescue a missing option. If the kind\\\\nyou would prefer is absent from a block\\'s allowed list, choose another supplied\\\\nkind for that block. If the list is empty, emit nothing for that block.\\\\n\\\\nReturn JSON exactly:\\\\n{\\\\n  \\\\\"nodes\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\"id\\\\\": \\\\\"string\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\"teaching_block_id\\\\\": \\\\\"<exact supplied block id>\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\"section_id\\\\\": \\\\\"<exact supplied section id>|null\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\"kind\\\\\": \\\\\"<one exact value from this block\\'s allowed_kinds>\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\"role\\\\\": \\\\\"string|null\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\"reason\\\\\": \\\\\"string\\\\\"\\\\n    }\\\\n  ]\\\\n}\\\\n\",\\n  \"knowledge\": {},\\n  \"modes\": [\\n    \"generate\"\\n  ],\\n  \"native_path\": \"learn\",\\n  \"payload_schema\": {\\n    \"additionalProperties\": false,\\n    \"properties\": {\\n      \"nodes\": {\\n        \"items\": {\\n          \"additionalProperties\": false,\\n          \"properties\": {\\n            \"id\": {\\n              \"minLength\": 1,\\n              \"type\": \"string\"\\n            },\\n            \"kind\": {\\n              \"enum\": [\\n                \"callout\",\\n                \"figure\",\\n                \"heading\",\\n                \"list\",\\n                \"paragraph\",\\n                \"table\"\\n              ],\\n              \"type\": \"string\"\\n            },\\n            \"reason\": {\\n              \"minLength\": 1,\\n              \"type\": \"string\"\\n            },\\n            \"role\": {\\n              \"type\": \"string\"\\n            },\\n            \"section_id\": {\\n              \"type\": \"string\"\\n            },\\n            \"teaching_block_id\": {\\n              \"minLength\": 1,\\n              \"type\": \"string\"\\n            }\\n          },\\n          \"required\": [\\n            \"id\",\\n            \"teaching_block_id\",\\n            \"kind\",\\n            \"reason\"\\n          ],\\n          \"type\": \"object\"\\n        },\\n        \"minItems\": 1,\\n        \"type\": \"array\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \"required\": [\\n      \"nodes\"\\n    ],\\n    \"type\": \"object\"\\n  },\\n  \"required_inputs\": [\\n    \"teaching_plan\",\\n    \"allowed_kinds_by_block\"\\n  ],\\n  \"validator_refs\": [\\n    \"document.composer_schema\"\\n  ]\\n}\\n\\n## SCOPED REQUEST\\n{\\n  \"inputs\": {\\n    \"allowed_kinds_by_block\": {\\n      \"check-b1\": [\\n        \"callout\",\\n        \"figure\",\\n        \"heading\",\\n        \"list\",\\n        \"paragraph\",\\n        \"table\"\\n      ],\\n      \"confront-b1\": [\\n        \"callout\",\\n        \"figure\",\\n        \"heading\",\\n        \"list\",\\n        \"paragraph\",\\n        \"table\"\\n      ],\\n      \"confront-b2\": [\\n        \"callout\",\\n        \"figure\",\\n        \"heading\",\\n        \"list\",\\n        \"paragraph\",\\n        \"table\"\\n      ],\\n      \"confront-b3\": [\\n        \"callout\",\\n        \"figure\",\\n        \"heading\",\\n        \"list\",\\n        \"paragraph\",\\n        \"table\"\\n      ],\\n      \"contrast-b1\": [\\n        \"callout\",\\n        \"figure\",\\n        \"heading\",\\n        \"list\",\\n        \"paragraph\",\\n        \"table\"\\n      ],\\n      \"contrast-b2\": [\\n        \"callout\",\\n        \"figure\",\\n        \"heading\",\\n        \"list\",\\n        \"paragraph\",\\n        \"table\"\\n      ],\\n      \"explain-b1\": [\\n        \"callout\",\\n        \"figure\",\\n        \"heading\",\\n        \"list\",\\n        \"paragraph\",\\n        \"table\"\\n      ],\\n      \"explain-b2\": [\\n        \"callout\",\\n        \"figure\",\\n        \"heading\",\\n        \"list\",\\n        \"paragraph\",\\n        \"table\"\\n      ],\\n      \"orient-b1\": [\\n        \"callout\",\\n        \"figure\",\\n        \"heading\",\\n        \"list\",\\n        \"paragraph\",\\n        \"table\"\\n      ],\\n      \"orient-b2\": [\\n        \"callout\",\\n        \"figure\",\\n        \"heading\",\\n        \"list\",\\n        \"paragraph\",\\n        \"table\"\\n      ]\\n    },\\n    \"teaching_plan\": {\\n      \"arc\": \"The lesson opens on the anchor passage, where one sentence is the writer\\'s claim and the others merely support it, and gives learners a reason to care about telling them apart. It builds the idea that a claim is the statement the writer asserts as true and wants readers to accept, then puts a topic sentence and a supporting fact side by side with the claim so the boundary is visible. It then surfaces the three beliefs that leak in \\\\u2014 that the claim is the topic, that any true sentence is the claim, and that the claim is always first \\\\u2014 and shows each failing on the same passage. It closes by having learners pick the writer\\'s claim from a fresh short passage on their own.\",\\n      \"revision\": 1,\\n      \"sections\": [\\n        {\\n          \"blocks\": [\\n            {\\n              \"allowed_kinds\": [\\n                \"callout\",\\n                \"figure\",\\n                \"heading\",\\n                \"list\",\\n                \"paragraph\",\\n                \"table\"\\n              ],\\n              \"brief\": \"Present the anchor passage as a short piece a reader actually meets. Point out that one sentence in it is the one the writer most wants the reader to accept, and that the surrounding sentences are doing something else \\\\u2014 giving reasons, examples, or background. Do not yet explain what a claim is, and do not name which sentence it is. End on the question of how a reader can tell which sentence carries that weight.\",\\n              \"evidence\": \"The anchor is a claim inside a real passage, so the opening must give the learner the felt problem of picking it out before any definition arrives; a definition first would turn the anchor into an illustration instead of the reason the lesson exists.\",\\n              \"id\": \"orient-b1\",\\n              \"intent\": \"orient\",\\n              \"learner_action\": null\\n            },\\n            {\\n              \"allowed_kinds\": [\\n                \"callout\",\\n                \"figure\",\\n                \"heading\",\\n                \"list\",\\n                \"paragraph\",\\n                \"table\"\\n              ],\\n              \"brief\": \"Recall that learners already read short passages and find details in them. Ask them what a reader does with details once they are found \\\\u2014 details sit in the passage to support something, not to be the point themselves. Do not define claim yet; the goal here is to surface the idea that a passage has a point that details serve.\",\\n              \"evidence\": \"Prior-1 and prior-2 establish passage reading and detail finding, which are the exact prerequisites the claim concept builds on; without making that dependence explicit the learner has no place to attach the new idea.\",\\n              \"id\": \"orient-b2\",\\n              \"intent\": \"activate-prior-knowledge\",\\n              \"learner_action\": null\\n            }\\n          ],\\n          \"slot_id\": \"orient\",\\n          \"specific_purpose\": \"Open attention on the anchor passage and establish that the sentences are doing different jobs, so learners want a way to tell the writer\\'s claim from the rest.\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"blocks\": [\\n            {\\n              \"allowed_kinds\": [\\n                \"callout\",\\n                \"figure\",\\n                \"heading\",\\n                \"list\",\\n                \"paragraph\",\\n                \"table\"\\n              ],\\n              \"brief\": \"State that the writer\\'s claim is the statement the writer asserts as true \\\\u2014 the one the writer wants the reader to accept, not merely the subject being discussed and not a detail offered in support. Use the anchor passage: identify the claim sentence and say in what way it asserts something the writer is putting forward, while the other sentences supply background, reasons, or examples. Do not classify sentences yet and do not warn about wrong picks yet.\",\\n              \"evidence\": \"The objective is stated as identifying the claim by saying what the writer asserts as true, so this slot must fix the meaning of claim precisely before any sentence can be judged; explanation of the passage alone would leave the term undefined.\",\\n              \"id\": \"explain-b1\",\\n              \"intent\": \"define\",\\n              \"learner_action\": null\\n            },\\n            {\\n              \"allowed_kinds\": [\\n                \"callout\",\\n                \"figure\",\\n                \"heading\",\\n                \"list\",\\n                \"paragraph\",\\n                \"table\"\\n              ],\\n              \"brief\": \"Explain why the writer bothers to state a claim at all: a short passage is written to move a reader toward accepting one statement, and the rest of the passage exists to make that statement credible. Return to the anchor and say what the writer wants the reader to accept, then say what the other sentences are doing to help. Do not introduce supporting reasons as a separate concept yet and do not name topic sentence or opening sentence.\",\\n              \"evidence\": \"The purpose of this slot is to build the idea itself and make it usable, which requires the learner to see the claim as the writer\\'s purpose for writing rather than as a label on one sentence; that mental model is what later lets the learner choose between candidate sentences.\",\\n              \"id\": \"explain-b2\",\\n              \"intent\": \"explain\",\\n              \"learner_action\": null\\n            }\\n          ],\\n          \"slot_id\": \"explain\",\\n          \"specific_purpose\": \"State what a writer\\'s claim is \\\\u2014 the statement the writer asserts as true and wants the reader to accept \\\\u2014 and ground it in the anchor passage so the definition is usable.\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"blocks\": [\\n            {\\n              \"allowed_kinds\": [\\n                \"callout\",\\n                \"figure\",\\n                \"heading\",\\n                \"list\",\\n                \"paragraph\",\\n                \"table\"\\n              ],\\n              \"brief\": \"Take three sentences from the anchor passage: one that merely names the topic being discussed, one that gives a supporting reason or fact, and the claim sentence. Contrast them on the single criterion of whether the sentence asserts something the writer is putting forward for the reader to accept. Make clear that all three are true statements about the same passage, so truth alone does not decide it, and that the topic sentence and the supporting fact are doing different jobs than the claim.\",\\n              \"evidence\": \"The contrast slot must expose the boundary of the idea, and the two most likely wrong candidates are the topic sentence and the supporting fact; comparing them with the claim on the acceptance criterion is what makes that boundary visible rather than asserted.\",\\n              \"id\": \"contrast-b1\",\\n              \"intent\": \"compare\",\\n              \"learner_action\": null\\n            },\\n            {\\n              \"allowed_kinds\": [\\n                \"callout\",\\n                \"figure\",\\n                \"heading\",\\n                \"list\",\\n                \"paragraph\",\\n                \"table\"\\n              ],\\n              \"brief\": \"Give the rule for sorting sentences: a sentence belongs to the writer\\'s claim only if the writer is asserting it as true and wants the reader to accept it; a sentence that names the subject or gives a supporting reason is not the claim. Use the anchor passage\\'s sentences as the sorting cases and have the learner place each one, guided by the rule just stated. Do not use sentences from outside the anchor here \\\\u2014 the sorting is on familiar ground.\",\\n              \"evidence\": \"Classification with a stated rule is the move that turns the comparison from something the teacher did into something the learner can do; it belongs after the side-by-side comparison so the rule has visible cases to bite on.\",\\n              \"id\": \"contrast-b2\",\\n              \"intent\": \"classify\",\\n              \"learner_action\": {\\n                \"action\": \"classify-items\",\\n                \"purpose\": \"require the learner to apply the acceptance criterion to real candidate sentences before the misconceptions are named\",\\n                \"target\": \"the sentences of the anchor passage, sorted into claim or not-claim\"\\n              }\\n            }\\n          ],\\n          \"slot_id\": \"contrast\",\\n          \"specific_purpose\": \"Place the claim beside its near misses \\\\u2014 a topic sentence and a supporting fact \\\\u2014 so the learner sees the boundary of the idea, not just the idea.\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"blocks\": [\\n            {\\n              \"allowed_kinds\": [\\n                \"callout\",\\n                \"figure\",\\n                \"heading\",\\n                \"list\",\\n                \"paragraph\",\\n                \"table\"\\n              ],\\n              \"brief\": \"State the belief that the writer\\'s claim is whatever the passage is about, so any sentence naming the topic counts. Show why it feels right: the topic sentence is true, prominent, and about the whole passage. Then fail it on the anchor passage \\\\u2014 the topic sentence names the subject, but the writer is not asking the reader to accept the subject, only the assertion in the claim sentence. Reinstate the criterion: the claim is what the writer asserts as true and wants accepted.\",\\n              \"evidence\": \"Misconception claim_is_the_topic is a held belief, not a slip, so it must be surfaced and failed on a case; the anchor passage has a topic sentence that this belief would confidently mislabel, which is exactly the case needed.\",\\n              \"id\": \"confront-b1\",\\n              \"intent\": \"diagnose-misconception\",\\n              \"learner_action\": null\\n            },\\n            {\\n              \"allowed_kinds\": [\\n                \"callout\",\\n                \"figure\",\\n                \"heading\",\\n                \"list\",\\n                \"paragraph\",\\n                \"table\"\\n              ],\\n              \"brief\": \"State the belief that every sentence stating something true is the writer\\'s claim, so a supporting fact counts. Show why it feels right: supporting facts are true, and they are about the same topic. Then fail it on the anchor passage \\\\u2014 the supporting fact is true and relevant, but its job is to back up the claim, and the writer is not asking the reader to accept the fact for its own sake. Keep the criterion visible: a claim is what the writer asserts and wants accepted, which a supporting fact is not.\",\\n              \"evidence\": \"Misconception claim_is_any_stated_fact cannot be handled by warning, because the learner\\'s error is a held model of what counts; the anchor\\'s supporting fact is a real true sentence that the model would select, so the failure is demonstrable here.\",\\n              \"id\": \"confront-b2\",\\n              \"intent\": \"diagnose-misconception\",\\n              \"learner_action\": null\\n            },\\n            {\\n              \"allowed_kinds\": [\\n                \"callout\",\\n                \"figure\",\\n                \"heading\",\\n                \"list\",\\n                \"paragraph\",\\n                \"table\"\\n              ],\\n              \"brief\": \"State the belief that the writer\\'s claim is always the opening sentence, so learners stop reading once they find sentence one. Show why it feels right: in many passages the opening does announce the point. Then fail it on the anchor passage \\\\u2014 check whether the first sentence is the one the writer is asking the reader to accept; in the anchor it is not, so the belief points at the wrong sentence. Close by restating that position in the passage cannot decide it; only the assertion role can.\",\\n              \"evidence\": \"Misconception claim_must_be_the_first_sentence is a shortcut belief that produces a confident but wrong pick; failing it on the anchor requires the learner to inspect the first sentence against the acceptance criterion rather than accept its position.\",\\n              \"id\": \"confront-b3\",\\n              \"intent\": \"diagnose-misconception\",\\n              \"learner_action\": null\\n            }\\n          ],\\n          \"slot_id\": \"confront\",\\n          \"specific_purpose\": \"Name the three beliefs that pull learners off the claim \\\\u2014 that it is the topic, that any true sentence is it, and that it is always first \\\\u2014 and show each failing on the anchor.\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"blocks\": [\\n            {\\n              \"allowed_kinds\": [\\n                \"callout\",\\n                \"figure\",\\n                \"heading\",\\n                \"list\",\\n                \"paragraph\",\\n                \"table\"\\n              ],\\n              \"brief\": \"Use one approved passage where the learner must decide which sentence states the writer\\'s claim. The passage is unfamiliar, so the answer must come from applying the criterion \\\\u2014 the sentence the writer asserts as true and wants accepted \\\\u2014 rather than from recalling the anchor. Do not add hints about position or topic, and do not return to the anchor passage.\",\\n              \"evidence\": \"The objective is to identify a writer\\'s claim in a short passage, so the check must use a passage the learner has not discussed; reusing the anchor would test recall of the lesson\\'s example rather than the objective itself.\",\\n              \"id\": \"check-b1\",\\n              \"intent\": \"check-understanding\",\\n              \"learner_action\": {\\n                \"action\": \"select-one\",\\n                \"purpose\": \"measure whether the learner can apply the acceptance criterion independently\",\\n                \"target\": \"which sentence in a fresh short passage states the writer\\'s claim\"\\n              }\\n            }\\n          ],\\n          \"slot_id\": \"check\",\\n          \"specific_purpose\": \"Measure whether the learner can find the writer\\'s claim in a fresh passage by applying the criterion, unaided by the anchor.\"\\n        }\\n      ],\\n      \"teaching_plan_id\": \"86096efd-501d-45d0-9b33-7101fd652b6a\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \"mode\": \"generate\",\\n  \"resolved_policy\": {\\n    \"allow_heuristic_composition_fallback\": true,\\n    \"closed_per_block_allowlists\": true\\n  },\\n  \"scoped_request\": {\\n    \"allow_heuristic_fallback\": true,\\n    \"allowed_kinds_by_block\": {\\n      \"check-b1\": [\\n        \"callout\",\\n        \"figure\",\\n        \"heading\",\\n        \"list\",\\n        \"paragraph\",\\n        \"table\"\\n      ],\\n      \"confront-b1\": [\\n        \"callout\",\\n        \"figure\",\\n        \"heading\",\\n        \"list\",\\n        \"paragraph\",\\n        \"table\"\\n      ],\\n      \"confront-b2\": [\\n        \"callout\",\\n        \"figure\",\\n        \"heading\",\\n        \"list\",\\n        \"paragraph\",\\n        \"table\"\\n      ],\\n      \"confront-b3\": [\\n        \"callout\",\\n        \"figure\",\\n        \"heading\",\\n        \"list\",\\n        \"paragraph\",\\n        \"table\"\\n      ],\\n      \"contrast-b1\": [\\n        \"callout\",\\n        \"figure\",\\n        \"heading\",\\n        \"list\",\\n        \"paragraph\",\\n        \"table\"\\n      ],\\n      \"contrast-b2\": [\\n        \"callout\",\\n        \"figure\",\\n        \"heading\",\\n        \"list\",\\n        \"paragraph\",\\n        \"table\"\\n      ],\\n      \"explain-b1\": [\\n        \"callout\",\\n        \"figure\",\\n        \"heading\",\\n        \"list\",\\n        \"paragraph\",\\n        \"table\"\\n      ],\\n      \"explain-b2\": [\\n        \"callout\",\\n        \"figure\",\\n        \"heading\",\\n        \"list\",\\n        \"paragraph\",\\n        \"table\"\\n      ],\\n      \"orient-b1\": [\\n        \"callout\",\\n        \"figure\",\\n        \"heading\",\\n        \"list\",\\n        \"paragraph\",\\n        \"table\"\\n      ],\\n      \"orient-b2\": [\\n        \"callout\",\\n        \"figure\",\\n        \"heading\",\\n        \"list\",\\n        \"paragraph\",\\n        \"table\"\\n      ]\\n    },\\n    \"path\": \"learn\",\\n    \"stage\": \"document_composition\"\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nReturn JSON only for this capability payload.'}, {'role': 'system', 'content': 'Return only valid JSON matching this schema.\\nDo not call tools.\\n{\"properties\": {\"response\": {\"additionalProperties\": true, \"type\": \"object\"}}, \"required\": [\"response\"], \"type\": \"object\", \"title\": \"dict\"}'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Return JSON only for the selected capability payload.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': '{\\n  \"nodes\": [\\n    {\\n      \"id\": \"orient-b1-n1\",\\n      \"teaching_block_id\": \"orient-b1\",\\n      \"section_id\": \"orient\",\\n      \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n      \"role\": \"anchor-passage\",\\n      \"reason\": \"Present the anchor passage as an authentic short piece the reader meets, ending on the felt problem of telling which sentence carries the weight; a paragraph keeps the anchor intact without pre-defining the claim.\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \"id\": \"orient-b1-n2\",\\n      \"teaching_block_id\": \"orient-b1\",\\n      \"section_id\": \"orient\",\\n      \"kind\": \"callout\",\\n      \"role\": \"orienting-question\",\\n      \"reason\": \"The brief ends on the question of how a reader can tell which sentence carries the writer\\'s weight; a callout isolates that question so the lesson has a deliberate open problem to resolve.\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \"id\": \"orient-b2-n1\",\\n      \"teaching_block_id\": \"orient-b2\",\\n      \"section_id\": \"orient\",\\n      \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n      \"role\": \"prior-knowledge-bridge\",\\n      \"reason\": \"Connects the learner\\'s existing passage-reading and detail-finding practice to the open question, surfacing the idea that a passage has a point the details serve, without yet naming the claim.\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \"id\": \"orient-b2-n2\",\\n      \"teaching_block_id\": \"orient-b2\",\\n      \"section_id\": \"orient\",\\n      \"kind\": \"callout\",\\n      \"role\": \"activation-prompt\",\\n      \"reason\": \"Prompts learners to articulate what a reader does with found details, making the prior dependence explicit rather than decorative.\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \"id\": \"explain-b1-n1\",\\n      \"teaching_block_id\": \"explain-b1\",\\n      \"section_id\": \"explain\",\\n      \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n      \"role\": \"definition\",\\n      \"reason\": \"States the claim definition \u2014 the statement the writer asserts as true and wants the reader to accept \u2014 distinguishing it from the subject and from supporting detail.\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \"id\": \"explain-b1-n2\",\\n      \"teaching_block_id\": \"explain-b1\",\\n      \"section_id\": \"explain\",\\n      \"kind\": \"callout\",\\n      \"role\": \"anchor-application\",\\n      \"reason\": \"Applies the definition to the anchor by identifying the claim sentence and saying in what way it asserts something the writer puts forward, while other sentences supply background, reasons, or examples.\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \"id\": \"explain-b2-n1\",\\n      \"teaching_block_id\": \"explain-b2\",\\n      \"section_id\": \"explain\",\\n      \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n      \"role\": \"rationale\",\\n      \"reason\": \"Explains why writers state a claim at all and what the rest of the passage is doing to make it credible, building the mental model that the claim is the writer\\'s purpose for writing.\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \"id\": \"explain-b2-n2\",\\n      \"teaching_block_id\": \"explain-b2\",\\n      \"section_id\": \"explain\",\\n      \"kind\": \"callout\",\\n      \"role\": \"anchor-return\",\\n      \"reason\": \"Returns to the anchor to say what the writer wants the reader to accept and what the other sentences are doing to help, keeping the definition usable without introducing new terminology.\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \"id\": \"contrast-b1-n1\",\\n      \"teaching_block_id\": \"contrast-b1\",\\n      \"section_id\": \"contrast\",\\n      \"kind\": \"table\",\\n      \"role\": \"side-by-side-contrast\",\\n      \"reason\": \"Places the topic sentence, the supporting fact, and the claim sentence side by side on the acceptance criterion, making the boundary visible and showing that truth alone does not decide it.\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \"id\": \"contrast-b1-n2\",\\n      \"teaching_block_id\": \"contrast-b1\",\\n      \"section_id\": \"contrast\",\\n      \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n      \"role\": \"boundary-statement\",\\n      \"reason\": \"Makes explicit that all three sentences are true statements about the same passage and that the topic sentence and supporting fact are doing different jobs than the claim.\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \"id\": \"contrast-b2-n1\",\\n      \"teaching_block_id\": \"contrast-b2\",\\n      \"section_id\": \"contrast\",\\n      \"kind\": \"callout\",\\n      \"role\": \"sorting-rule\",\\n      \"reason\": \"States the sorting rule plainly \u2014 a sentence counts as the claim only if the writer asserts it as true and wants the reader to accept it \u2014 so the classification task has a stated criterion to apply.\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \"id\": \"contrast-b2-n2\",\\n      \"teaching_block_id\": \"contrast-b2\",\\n      \"section_id\": \"contrast\",\\n      \"kind\": \"list\",\\n      \"role\": \"anchor-sorting-cases\",\\n      \"reason\": \"Lists the anchor passage\\'s sentences as the sorting cases for the classify-items learner action, keeping the classification on familiar ground and not outside the anchor.\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \"id\": \"confront-b1-n1\",\\n      \"teaching_block_id\": \"confront-b1\",\\n      \"section_id\": \"confront\",\\n      \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n      \"role\": \"misconception-statement\",\\n      \"reason\": \"States the belief that the claim is whatever the passage is about and shows why it feels right, surfacing the held belief rather than dismissing it.\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \"id\": \"confront-b1-n2\",\\n      \"teaching_block_id\": \"confront-b1\",\\n      \"section_id\": \"confront\",\\n      \"kind\": \"callout\",\\n      \"role\": \"anchor-failure\",\\n      \"reason\": \"Fails the misconception on the anchor \u2014 the topic sentence names the subject but the writer is not asking the reader to accept the subject \u2014 and reinstates the acceptance criterion.\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \"id\": \"confront-b2-n1\",\\n      \"teaching_block_id\": \"confront-b2\",\\n      \"section_id\": \"confront\",\\n      \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n      \"role\": \"misconception-statement\",\\n      \"reason\": \"States the belief that any true sentence is the claim and shows why it feels right, since supporting facts are true and on the same topic.\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \"id\": \"confront-b2-n2\",\\n      \"teaching_block_id\": \"confront-b2\",\\n      \"section_id\": \"confront\",\\n      \"kind\": \"callout\",\\n      \"role\": \"anchor-failure\",\\n      \"reason\": \"Fails the misconception on the anchor: the supporting fact is true and relevant, but its job is to back the claim, and the writer is not asking the reader to accept it for its own sake.\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \"id\": \"confront-b3-n1\",\\n      \"teaching_block_id\": \"confront-b3\",\\n      \"section_id\": \"confront\",\\n      \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n      \"role\": \"misconception-statement\",\\n      \"reason\": \"States the belief that the claim is always the opening sentence and shows why it feels right, since many passages announce their point first.\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \"id\": \"confront-b3-n2\",\\n      \"teaching_block_id\": \"confront-b3\",\\n      \"section_id\": \"confront\",\\n      \"kind\": \"callout\",\\n      \"role\": \"anchor-failure\",\\n      \"reason\": \"Fails the shortcut by checking the anchor\\'s first sentence against the acceptance criterion and showing it is not the sentence the writer asks the reader to accept, then closes by restating that position cannot decide it.\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \"id\": \"check-b1-n1\",\\n      \"teaching_block_id\": \"check-b1\",\\n      \"section_id\": \"check\",\\n      \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n      \"role\": \"check-framing\",\\n      \"reason\": \"Sets up the fresh approved passage and states that the learner must decide which sentence states the writer\\'s claim by applying the criterion, adding no hints about position or topic.\"\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '1 validation error:\\n```json\\n[\\n  {\\n    \"type\": \"missing\",\\n    \"loc\": [\\n      \"response\"\\n    ],\\n    \"msg\": \"Field required\"\\n  }\\n]\\n```\\n\\nFix the errors and try again.'}], 'model': 'deepseek-flash', 'max_completion_tokens': 8000, 'stream': False}, 'extra_json': {'thinking': {'type': 'disabled'}}}"}
