riter\\'s claim and the others merely support it, and gives learners a reason to care about telling them apart. It builds the idea that a claim is the statement the writer asserts as true and wants readers to accept, then puts a topic sentence and a supporting fact side by side with the claim so the boundary is visible. It then surfaces the three beliefs that leak in \\\\u2014 that the claim is the topic, that any true sentence is the claim, and that the claim is always first \\\\u2014 and shows each failing on the same passage. It closes by having learners pick the writer\\'s claim from a fresh short passage on their own.\"\\n    },\\n    \"neighbours\": [\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Connects the learner\\'s existing passage-reading and detail-finding practice to the open question, surfacing the idea that a passage has a point the details serve, without yet naming the claim.\",\\n        \"role\": \"prior-knowledge-bridge\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"orient-b2\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"callout\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Prompts learners to articulate what a reader does with found details, making the prior dependence explicit rather than decorative.\",\\n        \"role\": \"activation-prompt\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"orient-b2\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"callout\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Applies the definition to the anchor by identifying the claim sentence and saying in what way it asserts something the writer puts forward, while other sentences supply background, reasons, or examples.\",\\n        \"role\": \"anchor-application\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"explain-b1\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Explains why writers state a claim at all and what the rest of the passage is doing to make it credible, building the mental model that the claim is the writer\\'s purpose for writing.\",\\n        \"role\": \"rationale\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"explain-b2\"\\n      }\\n    ],\\n    \"reason\": \"States the claim definition \\\\u2014 the statement the writer asserts as true and wants the reader to accept \\\\u2014 distinguishing it from the subject and from supporting detail.\",\\n    \"role\": \"definition\",\\n    \"sourcebook_entries\": [],\\n    \"teaching_block\": {\\n      \"brief\": \"State that the writer\\'s claim is the statement the writer asserts as true \\\\u2014 the one the writer wants the reader to accept, not merely the subject being discussed and not a detail offered in support. Use the anchor passage: identify the claim sentence and say in what way it asserts something the writer is putting forward, while the other sentences supply background, reasons, or examples. Do not classify sentences yet and do not warn about wrong picks yet.\",\\n      \"evidence\": \"The objective is stated as identifying the claim by saying what the writer asserts as true, so this slot must fix the meaning of claim precisely before any sentence can be judged; explanation of the passage alone would leave the term undefined.\",\\n      \"id\": \"explain-b1\",\\n      \"intent\": \"define\",\\n      \"sourcebook_refs\": []\\n    },\\n    \"terminology\": [\\n      \"claim\",\\n      \"evidence\",\\n      \"relevant evidence\",\\n      \"irrelevant detail\",\\n      \"support\"\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  \"mode\": \"generate\",\\n  \"scoped_request\": {\\n    \"allowed_facts\": [\\n      \"read and understand a short passage\",\\n      \"find details in a text\",\\n      \"A claim is what the writer asserts as true.\",\\n      \"The claim can be stated in a single sentence.\",\\n      \"Recognizing a writer\\'s claim in a short passage\",\\n      \"Distinguishing evidence that directly supports a claim from unrelated details\",\\n      \"Explaining the link between selected evidence and the claim\"\\n    ],\\n    \"brief\": \"State that the writer\\'s claim is the statement the wr