ent primitive.\\\\n\\\\nAllowed kinds: paragraph, heading, list, table, callout, figure.\\\\n(Figure: write caption + alt only. Asset generation uses the figure pipeline.)\\\\n\\\\nHard rules:\\\\n- Fill ONLY fields for the assigned kind. No unrelated fields.\\\\n- No component_id, template_id, page_break, ruled_lines, or media objects.\\\\n- Do not author interactions; those use the interaction writer.\\\\n- The Teaching Plan brief is a WRITER INSTRUCTION, not student-facing text.\\\\n  Never copy the brief verbatim into the learner-facing fields.\\\\n- Honour objective, terminology, evidence / approved facts, and must_not_introduce.\\\\n- Use supplied Sourcebook entries exactly for bound values, facts, dates, names,\\\\n  labels, units, examples, and stimuli. Never silently replace a bound example.\\\\n- Do not author response tasks; those are shared TaskSpecs consumed by both paths.\\\\n- Do not reteach prior_established content; reference it briefly when useful.\\\\n- Stay coherent with neighbouring composition choices when provided.\\\\n- Do not invent placeholder rows (\\\\\"Row 1\\\\\"), generic headers (\\\\\"Item\\\\\"/\\\\\"Detail\\\\\"),\\\\n  or \\\\\"Content pending.\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\nPrimitive field contracts:\\\\n- paragraph \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"text\\\\\": string }\\\\n- heading \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"text\\\\\": string, \\\\\"level\\\\\": 1|2|3 }\\\\n- list \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"ordered\\\\\": boolean, \\\\\"items\\\\\": [string, ...] }\\\\n- table \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"headers\\\\\": [string, ...], \\\\\"rows\\\\\": [[string, ...], ...], \\\\\"caption\\\\\": string }\\\\n- callout \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"tone\\\\\": \\\\\"note\\\\\"|\\\\\"warning\\\\\"|\\\\\"tip\\\\\"|\\\\\"important\\\\\", \\\\\"title\\\\\": string, \\\\\"body\\\\\": string }\\\\n- figure \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"caption\\\\\": string, \\\\\"alt\\\\\": string }\\\\n\\\\nReturn JSON matching the assigned primitive schema only (plus kind).\\\\n\",\\n  \"knowledge\": {},\\n  \"modes\": [\\n    \"generate\"\\n  ],\\n  \"native_path\": \"document\",\\n  \"payload_schema\": {\\n    \"additionalProperties\": false,\\n    \"properties\": {\\n      \"kind\": {\\n        \"const\": \"paragraph\"\\n      },\\n      \"text\": {\\n        \"minLength\": 1,\\n        \"type\": \"string\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \"required\": [\\n      \"kind\",\\n      \"text\"\\n    ],\\n    \"type\": \"object\"\\n  },\\n  \"required_inputs\": [\\n    \"kind\",\\n    \"brief\",\\n    \"teaching_block\"\\n  ],\\n  \"validator_refs\": [\\n    \"document.writer_schema\"\\n  ]\\n}\\n\\n## SCOPED REQUEST\\n{\\n  \"inputs\": {\\n    \"allowed_facts\": [\\n      \"read and understand a short passage\",\\n      \"find details in a text\",\\n      \"A claim is what the writer asserts as true.\",\\n      \"The claim can be stated in a single sentence.\",\\n      \"Recognizing a writer\\'s claim in a short passage\",\\n      \"Distinguishing evidence that directly supports a claim from unrelated details\",\\n      \"Explaining the link between selected evidence and the claim\"\\n    ],\\n    \"brief\": \"Explain why the writer bothers to state a claim at all: a short passage is written to move a reader toward accepting one statement, and the rest of the passage exists to make that statement credible. Return to the anchor and say what the writer wants the reader to accept, then say what the other sentences are doing to help. Do not introduce supporting reasons as a separate concept yet and do not name topic sentence or opening sentence.\",\\n    \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n    \"lesson_context\": {\\n      \"input_availability\": \"supplied_statements\",\\n      \"objective\": \"Identify the writer\\'s claim in a short passage by stating what the writer is asserting as true.\",\\n      \"sourcebook_entries\": [],\\n      \"subject\": \"English Language Arts\",\\n      \"teaching_plan_revision\": 1,\\n      \"title\": \"The lesson opens on the anchor passage, where one sentence is the writer\\'s claim and the others merely support it, and gives learners a reason to care about telling them apart. It builds the idea that a claim is the statement the writer asserts as true and wants readers to accept, then puts a topic sentence and a supporting fact side by side with the claim so the boundary is visible. It then surfaces the three beliefs that leak in \\\\u2014 that the claim is the topic, that any true sentence is the claim, and that the claim is always first \\\\u2014 and shows each failing on the same passage. It closes by having learners pick the writer\\'s claim from a fresh short passage on their own.\"\\n    },\\n    \"neighbours\": [\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n        \"reason\": \"States the claim definition \\\\u2014 the statement the writer asserts as true and wants the reader to accept \\\\u2014 distinguishing it from the subject and from supporting detail.\",\\n        \"role\": \"definition\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"explain-b1\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"callout\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Applies the definition to the anchor by identifying the claim sentence and saying in what way it asserts something the writer puts forward, while other sentences supply background, reasons, or examples.\",\\n        \"role\": \"anchor-application\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"explain-b1\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"callout\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Returns to the anchor to say what the writer wants the reader to accept and what the other sentences are doing to help, keeping the definition usable without introducing new terminology.\",\\n        \"role\": \"anchor-return\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"explain-b2\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"table\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Places the topic sentence, the supporting fact, and the claim sentence side by side on the acceptance criterion, making the boundary visible and showing that truth alone does not decide it.\",\\n        \"role\": \"side-by-side-contrast\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"contrast-b1\"\\n      }\\n    ],\\n    \"reason\": \"Explains why writers state a claim at all and what the rest of the passage is doing to make it credible, building the mental model that the claim is the writer\\'s purpose for writing.\",\\n    \"role\": \"rationale\",\\n    \"sourcebook_entries\": [],\\n    \"teaching_block\": {\\n      \"brief\": \"Explain why the writer bothers to state a claim at all: a short passage is written to move a reader toward accepting one statement, and the rest of the passage exists to make that statement credible. Return to the anchor and say what the writer wants the reader to accept, then say what the other sentences are doing to help. Do not introduce supporting reasons as a separate concept yet and do not name topic sentence or opening sentence.\",\\n      \"evidence\": \"The purpose of this slot is to build the idea itself and make it usable, which requires the learner to see the claim as the writer\\'s purpose for writing rather than as a label on one sentence; that mental model is what later lets the learner choose between candidate sentences.\",\\n      \"id\": \"explain-b2\",\\n      \"intent\": \"explain\",\\n      \"sourcebook_refs\": []\\n    },\\n    \"terminology\": [\\n      \"claim\",\\n      \"evidence\",\\n      \"relevant evidence\",\\n      \"irrelevant detail\",\\n      \"support\"\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  \"mode\": \"generate\",\\n  \"scoped_request\": {\\n    \"allowed_facts\": [\\n      \"read and understand a short passage\",\\n      \"find details in a text\",\\n      \"A claim is what the writer asserts as true.\",\\n      \"The claim can be stated in a single sentence.\",\\n      \"Recognizing a writer\\'s claim in a short passage\",\\n      \"Distinguishing evidence that directly supports a claim from unrelated details\",\\n      \"Explaining the link between selected evidence and the claim\"\\n    ],\\n    \"brief\": \"Explain why the writer bothers to state a claim at all: a short passage is written to move a reader toward accepting one statement, and the rest of the passage exists to make that statement credible. Return to the anchor and say what the writer wants the reader to accept, then say what the other sentences are doing to help. Do not introduce supporting reasons as a separate concept yet and do not name topic sentence or opening sentence.\",\\n    \"evidence\": \"The purpose of this slot is to build the idea itself and make it usable, which requires the learner to see the claim as the writer\\'s purpose for writing rather than as a label on one sentence; that mental model is what later lets the learner choose between candidate sentences.\",\\n    \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n    \"lesson_context\": {\\n      \"input_availability\": \"supplied_statements\",\\n      \"objective\": \"Identify the writer\\'s claim in a short passage by stating what the writer is asserting as true.\",\\n      \"sourcebook_entries\": [],\\n      \"subject\": \"English Language Arts\",\\n      \"teaching_plan_revision\": 1,\\n      \"title\": \"The lesson opens on the anchor passage, where one sentence is the writer\\'s claim and the others merely support it, and gives learners a reason to care about telling them apart. It builds the idea that a claim is the statement the writer asserts as true and wants readers to accept, then puts a topic sentence and a supporting fact side by side with the claim so the boundary is visible. It then surfaces the three beliefs that leak in \\\\u2014 that the claim is the topic, that any true sentence is the claim, and that the claim is always first \\\\u2014 and shows each failing on the same passage. It closes by having learners pick the writer\\'s claim from a fresh short passage on their own.\"\\n    },\\n    \"neighbours\": [\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n        \"reason\": \"States the claim definition \\\\u2014 the statement the writer asserts as true and wants the reader to accept \\\\u2014 distinguishing it from the subject and from supporting detail.\",\\n        \"role\": \"definition\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"explain-b1\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"callout\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Applies the definition to the anchor by identifying the claim sentence and saying in what way it asserts something the writer puts forward, while other sentences supply background, reasons, or examples.\",\\n        \"role\": \"anchor-application\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"explain-b1\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"callout\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Returns to the anchor to say what the writer wants the reader to accept and what the other sentences are doing to help, keeping the definition usable without introducing new terminology.\",\\n        \"role\": \"anchor-return\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"explain-b2\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"table\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Places the topic sentence, the supporting fact, and the claim sentence side by side on the acceptance criterion, making the boundary visible and showing that truth alone does not decide it.\",\\n        \"role\": \"side-by-side-contrast\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"contrast-b1\"\\n      }\\n    ],\\n    \"objective\": \"Identify the writer\\'s claim in a short passage by stating what the writer is asserting as true.\",\\n    \"reason\": \"Explains why writers state a claim at all and what the rest of the passage is doing to make it credible, building the mental model that the claim is the writer\\'s purpose for writing.\",\\n    \"role\": \"rationale\",\\n    \"sourcebook_entries\": [],\\n    \"stage\": \"document_writing\",\\n    \"teaching_block\": {\\n      \"brief\": \"Explain why the writer bothers to state a claim at all: a short passage is written to move a reader toward accepting one statement, and the rest of the passage exists to make that statement credible. Return to the anchor and say what the writer wants the reader to accept, then say what the other sentences are doing to help. Do not introduce supporting reasons as a separate concept yet and do not name topic sentence or opening sentence.\",\\n      \"evidence\": \"The purpose of this slot is to build the idea itself and make it usable, which requires the learner to see the claim as the writer\\'s purpose for writing rather than as a label on one sentence; that mental model is what later lets the learner choose between candidate sentences.\",\\n      \"id\": \"explain-b2\",\\n      \"intent\": \"explain\",\\n      \"sourcebook_refs\": []\\n    },\\n    \"terminology\": [\\n      \"claim\",\\n      \"evidence\",\\n      \"relevant evidence\",\\n      \"irrelevant detail\",\\n      \"support\"\\n    ]\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nReturn JSON only for this capability payload.'}, {'role': 'system', 'content': 'Return only valid JSON matching this schema.\\nDo not call tools.\\n{\"properties\": {\"response\": {\"additionalProperties\": true, \"type\": \"object\"}}, \"required\": [\"response\"], \"type\": \"object\", \"title\": \"dict\"}'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Return JSON only for the selected capability payload.'}], 'model': 'deepseek-flash', 'max_completion_tokens': 8000, 'stream': False}, 'extra_json': {'thinking': {'type': 'disabled'}}}"}
