{"timestamp": "2026-09-16T16:42:06.365718+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "logger": "openai._base_client", "message": "Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'headers': {'User-Agent': 'pydantic-ai/1.107.1'}, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-d2bbc825-0281-4473-a19f-1b30a7cc1a65', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You author one already-selected educational capability. Follow the\\nsupplied AuthoringDefinition and exact output schema. Preserve block identity, objective, scope,\\nlearner action, source ownership and assessment mode. Do not select another capability or redesign\\nthe teaching plan.\\n\\nPlanning instructions are not final content. For an activity, produce a meaningful prompt, task\\ndata, justified answer relationships and useful feedback as required by the contract. Do not infer\\ncorrectness from option positions, number/word positions, example ordering or arbitrary defaults.\\nDo not copy illustrative examples as unrelated lesson content.\\n\\nTreat approved items as authoritative content. Conversion must preserve their question and answer\\nmeaning. Report incompatibility through the defined error path instead of guessing. Use supported\\nteacher-review only when the work order policy permits it.\\n\\nReturn exactly the selected output schema. Do not emit sibling schemas, markdown wrappers, internal\\nplanning metadata or extra fields. If required information is absent, use the configured\\nmissing-input mechanism; do not create placeholder content to satisfy structure.\\n\\nDo not invent or overwrite knowledge/assessment policy fields. Effective policy is code-owned.\\n\\n## KNOWLEDGE POLICY\\nKnowledge policy: use supplied facts as the primary basis when present; do not describe model knowledge as approved factual material.\\n\\n## PACKAGE CAPABILITY INSTRUCTIONS\\nYou write LEARNER-FACING content for ONE ordinary document primitive.\\n\\nAllowed kinds: paragraph, heading, list, table, callout, figure.\\n(Figure: write caption + alt only. Asset generation uses the figure pipeline.)\\n\\nHard rules:\\n- Fill ONLY fields for the assigned kind. No unrelated fields.\\n- No component_id, template_id, page_break, ruled_lines, or media objects.\\n- Do not author interactions; those use the interaction writer.\\n- The Teaching Plan brief is a WRITER INSTRUCTION, not student-facing text.\\n  Never copy the brief verbatim into the learner-facing fields.\\n- Honour objective, terminology, evidence / approved facts, and must_not_introduce.\\n- Use supplied Sourcebook entries exactly for bound values, facts, dates, names,\\n  labels, units, examples, and stimuli. Never silently replace a bound example.\\n- Do not author response tasks; those are shared TaskSpecs consumed by both paths.\\n- Do not reteach prior_established content; reference it briefly when useful.\\n- Stay coherent with neighbouring composition choices when provided.\\n- Do not invent placeholder rows (\"Row 1\"), generic headers (\"Item\"/\"Detail\"),\\n  or \"Content pending.\"\\n\\nPrimitive field contracts:\\n- paragraph \u2192 { \"text\": string }\\n- heading \u2192 { \"text\": string, \"level\": 1|2|3 }\\n- list \u2192 { \"ordered\": boolean, \"items\": [string, ...] }\\n- table \u2192 { \"headers\": [string, ...], \"rows\": [[string, ...], ...], \"caption\": string }\\n- callout \u2192 { \"tone\": \"note\"|\"warning\"|\"tip\"|\"important\", \"title\": string, \"body\": string }\\n- figure \u2192 { \"caption\": string, \"alt\": string }\\n\\nReturn JSON matching the assigned primitive schema only (plus kind).\\n\\n\\n## AUTHORING DEFINITION\\n{\\n  \"assessment\": {},\\n  \"capability_id\": \"document-writer:callout\",\\n  \"converter_ref\": null,\\n  \"definition_hash\": \"22fefe3ecb3c07b4dd776eba11349b83db5548bf3b00e3b39d96e9d0f85fda3b:callout\",\\n  \"instructions\": \"You write LEARNER-FACING content for ONE ordinary document primitive.\\\\n\\\\nAllowed kinds: paragraph, heading, list, table, callout, figure.\\\\n(Figure: write caption + alt only. Asset generation uses the figure pipeline.)\\\\n\\\\nHard rules:\\\\n- Fill ONLY fields for the assigned kind. No unrelated fields.\\\\n- No component_id, template_id, page_break, ruled_lines, or media objects.\\\\n- Do not author interactions; those use the interaction writer.\\\\n- The Teaching Plan brief is a WRITER INSTRUCTION, not student-facing text.\\\\n  Never copy the brief verbatim into the learner-facing fields.\\\\n- Honour objective, terminology, evidence / approved facts, and must_not_introduce.\\\\n- Use supplied Sourcebook entries exactly for bound values, facts, dates, names,\\\\n  labels, units, examples, and stimuli. Never silently replace a bound example.\\\\n- Do not author response tasks; those are shared TaskSpecs consumed by both paths.\\\\n- Do not reteach prior_established content; reference it briefly when useful.\\\\n- Stay coherent with neighbouring composition choices when provided.\\\\n- Do not invent placeholder rows (\\\\\"Row 1\\\\\"), generic headers (\\\\\"Item\\\\\"/\\\\\"Detail\\\\\"),\\\\n  or \\\\\"Content pending.\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\nPrimitive field contracts:\\\\n- paragraph \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"text\\\\\": string }\\\\n- heading \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"text\\\\\": string, \\\\\"level\\\\\": 1|2|3 }\\\\n- list \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"ordered\\\\\": boolean, \\\\\"items\\\\\": [string, ...] }\\\\n- table \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"headers\\\\\": [string, ...], \\\\\"rows\\\\\": [[string, ...], ...], \\\\\"caption\\\\\": string }\\\\n- callout \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"tone\\\\\": \\\\\"note\\\\\"|\\\\\"warning\\\\\"|\\\\\"tip\\\\\"|\\\\\"important\\\\\", \\\\\"title\\\\\": string, \\\\\"body\\\\\": string }\\\\n- figure \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"caption\\\\\": string, \\\\\"alt\\\\\": string }\\\\n\\\\nReturn JSON matching the assigned primitive schema only (plus kind).\\\\n\",\\n  \"knowledge\": {},\\n  \"modes\": [\\n    \"generate\"\\n  ],\\n  \"native_path\": \"document\",\\n  \"payload_schema\": {\\n    \"additionalProperties\": false,\\n    \"properties\": {\\n      \"body\": {\\n        \"minLength\": 1,\\n        \"type\": \"string\"\\n      },\\n      \"kind\": {\\n        \"const\": \"callout\"\\n      },\\n      \"title\": {\\n        \"type\": \"string\"\\n      },\\n      \"tone\": {\\n        \"enum\": [\\n          \"note\",\\n          \"warning\",\\n          \"tip\",\\n          \"important\"\\n        ],\\n        \"type\": \"string\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \"required\": [\\n      \"kind\",\\n      \"tone\",\\n      \"body\"\\n    ],\\n    \"type\": \"object\"\\n  },\\n  \"required_inputs\": [\\n    \"kind\",\\n    \"brief\",\\n    \"teaching_block\"\\n  ],\\n  \"validator_refs\": [\\n    \"document.writer_schema\"\\n  ]\\n}\\n\\n## SCOPED REQUEST\\n{\\n  \"inputs\": {\\n    \"allowed_facts\": [\\n      \"read and understand a short passage\",\\n      \"find details in a text\",\\n      \"A claim is what the writer asserts as true.\",\\n      \"The claim can be stated in a single sentence.\",\\n      \"Recognizing a writer\\'s claim in a short passage\",\\n      \"Distinguishing evidence that directly supports a claim from unrelated details\",\\n      \"Explaining the link between selected evidence and the claim\"\\n    ],\\n    \"brief\": \"Give the rule for sorting sentences: a sentence belongs to the writer\\'s claim only if the writer is asserting it as true and wants the reader to accept it; a sentence that names the subject or gives a supporting reason is not the claim. Use the anchor passage\\'s sentences as the sorting cases and have the learner place each one, guided by the rule just stated. Do not use sentences from outside the anchor here \\\\u2014 the sorting is on familiar ground.\",\\n    \"kind\": \"callout\",\\n    \"lesson_context\": {\\n      \"input_availability\": \"supplied_statements\",\\n      \"objective\": \"Identify the writer\\'s claim in a short passage by stating what the writer is asserting as true.\",\\n      \"sourcebook_entries\": [],\\n      \"subject\": \"English Language Arts\",\\n      \"teaching_plan_revision\": 1,\\n      \"title\": \"The lesson opens on the anchor passage, where one sentence is the writer\\'s claim and the others merely support it, and gives learners a reason to care about telling them apart. It builds the idea that a claim is the statement the writer asserts as true and wants readers to accept, then puts a topic sentence and a supporting fact side by side with the claim so the boundary is visible. It then surfaces the three beliefs that leak in \\\\u2014 that the claim is the topic, that any true sentence is the claim, and that the claim is always first \\\\u2014 and shows each failing on the same passage. It closes by having learners pick the writer\\'s claim from a fresh short passage on their own.\"\\n    },\\n    \"neighbours\": [\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"table\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Places the topic sentence, the supporting fact, and the claim sentence side by side on the acceptance criterion, making the boundary visible and showing that truth alone does not decide it.\",\\n        \"role\": \"side-by-side-contrast\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"contrast-b1\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Makes explicit that all three sentences are true statements about the same passage and that the topic sentence and supporting fact are doing different jobs than the claim.\",\\n        \"role\": \"boundary-statement\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"contrast-b1\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"list\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Lists the anchor passage\\'s sentences as the sorting cases for the classify-items learner action, keeping the classification on familiar ground and not outside the anchor.\",\\n        \"role\": \"anchor-sorting-cases\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"contrast-b2\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n        \"reason\": \"States the belief that the claim is whatever the passage is about and shows why it feels right, surfacing the held belief rather than dismissing it.\",\\n        \"role\": \"misconception-statement\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"confront-b1\"\\n      }\\n    ],\\n    \"reason\": \"States the sorting rule plainly \\\\u2014 a sentence counts as the claim only if the writer asserts it as true and wants the reader to accept it \\\\u2014 so the classification task has a stated criterion to apply.\",\\n    \"role\": \"sorting-rule\",\\n    \"sourcebook_entries\": [],\\n    \"teaching_block\": {\\n      \"brief\": \"Give the rule for sorting sentences: a sentence belongs to the writer\\'s claim only if the writer is asserting it as true and wants the reader to accept it; a sentence that names the subject or gives a supporting reason is not the claim. Use the anchor passage\\'s sentences as the sorting cases and have the learner place each one, guided by the rule just stated. Do not use sentences from outside the anchor here \\\\u2014 the sorting is on familiar ground.\",\\n      \"evidence\": \"Classification with a stated rule is the move that turns the comparison from something the teacher did into something the learner can do; it belongs after the side-by-side comparison so the rule has visible cases to bite on.\",\\n      \"id\": \"contrast-b2\",\\n      \"intent\": \"classify\",\\n      \"sourcebook_refs\": []\\n    },\\n    \"terminology\": [\\n      \"claim\",\\n      \"evidence\",\\n      \"relevant evidence\",\\n      \"irrelevant detail\",\\n      \"support\"\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  \"mode\": \"generate\",\\n  \"scoped_request\": {\\n    \"allowed_facts\": [\\n      \"read and understand a short passage\",\\n      \"find details in a text\",\\n      \"A claim is what the writer asserts as true.\",\\n      \"The claim can be stated in a single sentence.\",\\n      \"Recognizing a writer\\'s claim in a short passage\",\\n      \"Distinguishing evidence that directly supports a claim from unrelated details\",\\n      \"Explaining the link between selected evidence and the claim\"\\n    ],\\n    \"brief\": \"Give the rule for sorting sentences: a sentence belongs to the writer\\'s claim only if the writer is asserting it as true and wants the reader to accept it; a sentence that names the subject or gives a supporting reason is not the claim. Use the anchor passage\\'s sentences as the sorting cases and have the learner place each one, guided by the rule just stated. Do not use sentences from outside the anchor here \\\\u2014 the sorting is on familiar ground.\",\\n    \"evidence\": \"Classification with a stated rule is the move that turns the comparison from something the teacher did into something the learner can do; it belongs after the side-by-side comparison so the rule has visible cases to bite on.\",\\n    \"kind\": \"callout\",\\n    \"lesson_context\": {\\n      \"input_availability\": \"supplied_statements\",\\n      \"objective\": \"Identify the writer\\'s claim in a short passage by stating what the writer is asserting as true.\",\\n      \"sourcebook_entries\": [],\\n      \"subject\": \"English Language Arts\",\\n      \"teaching_plan_revision\": 1,\\n      \"title\": \"The lesson opens on the anchor passage, where one sentence is the writer\\'s claim and the others merely support it, and gives learners a reason to care about telling them apart. It builds the idea that a claim is the statement the writer asserts as true and wants readers to accept, then puts a topic sentence and a supporting fact side by side with the claim so the boundary is visible. It then surfaces the three beliefs that leak in \\\\u2014 that the claim is the topic, that any true sentence is the claim, and that the claim is always first \\\\u2014 and shows each failing on the same passage. It closes by having learners pick the writer\\'s claim from a fresh short passage on their own.\"\\n    },\\n    \"neighbours\": [\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"table\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Places the topic sentence, the supporting fact, and the claim sentence side by side on the acceptance criterion, making the boundary visible and showing that truth alone does not decide it.\",\\n        \"role\": \"side-by-side-contrast\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"contrast-b1\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Makes explicit that all three sentences are true statements about the same passage and that the topic sentence and supporting fact are doing different jobs than the claim.\",\\n        \"role\": \"boundary-statement\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"contrast-b1\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"list\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Lists the anchor passage\\'s sentences as the sorting cases for the classify-items learner action, keeping the classification on familiar ground and not outside the anchor.\",\\n        \"role\": \"anchor-sorting-cases\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"contrast-b2\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n        \"reason\": \"States the belief that the claim is whatever the passage is about and shows why it feels right, surfacing the held belief rather than dismissing it.\",\\n        \"role\": \"misconception-statement\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"confront-b1\"\\n      }\\n    ],\\n    \"objective\": \"Identify the writer\\'s claim in a short passage by stating what the writer is asserting as true.\",\\n    \"reason\": \"States the sorting rule plainly \\\\u2014 a sentence counts as the claim only if the writer asserts it as true and wants the reader to accept it \\\\u2014 so the classification task has a stated criterion to apply.\",\\n    \"role\": \"sorting-rule\",\\n    \"sourcebook_entries\": [],\\n    \"stage\": \"document_writing\",\\n    \"teaching_block\": {\\n      \"brief\": \"Give the rule for sorting sentences: a sentence belongs to the writer\\'s claim only if the writer is asserting it as true and wants the reader to accept it; a sentence that names the subject or gives a supporting reason is not the claim. Use the anchor passage\\'s sentences as the sorting cases and have the learner place each one, guided by the rule just stated. Do not use sentences from outside the anchor here \\\\u2014 the sorting is on familiar ground.\",\\n      \"evidence\": \"Classification with a stated rule is the move that turns the comparison from something the teacher did into something the learner can do; it belongs after the side-by-side comparison so the rule has visible cases to bite on.\",\\n      \"id\": \"contrast-b2\",\\n      \"intent\": \"classify\",\\n      \"sourcebook_refs\": []\\n    },\\n    \"terminology\": [\\n      \"claim\",\\n      \"evidence\",\\n      \"relevant evidence\",\\n      \"irrelevant detail\",\\n      \"support\"\\n    ]\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nReturn JSON only for this capability payload.'}, {'role': 'system', 'content': 'Return only valid JSON matching this schema.\\nDo not call tools.\\n{\"properties\": {\"response\": {\"additionalProperties\": true, \"type\": \"object\"}}, \"required\": [\"response\"], \"type\": \"object\", \"title\": \"dict\"}'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Return JSON only for the selected capability payload.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': '{\\n  \"kind\": \"callout\",\\n  \"tone\": \"important\",\\n  \"title\": \"The sorting rule\",\\n  \"body\": \"Place each sentence from the anchor passage using this rule: a sentence belongs to the writer\\'s claim only if the writer is asserting it as true and wants you to accept it. A sentence that names the subject is not the claim. A sentence that gives a supporting reason is not the claim either \u2014 it is there to help the claim, not to be it.\"\\n}'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '1 validation error:\\n```json\\n[\\n  {\\n    \"type\": \"missing\",\\n    \"loc\": [\\n      \"response\"\\n    ],\\n    \"msg\": \"Field required\"\\n  }\\n]\\n```\\n\\nFix the errors and try again.'}], 'model': 'deepseek-flash', 'max_completion_tokens': 8000, 'stream': False}, 'extra_json': {'thinking': {'type': 'disabled'}}}"}
