ne sentence is the writer\\'s claim and the others merely support it, and gives learners a reason to care about telling them apart. It builds the idea that a claim is the statement the writer asserts as true and wants readers to accept, then puts a topic sentence and a supporting fact side by side with the claim so the boundary is visible. It then surfaces the three beliefs that leak in \\\\u2014 that the claim is the topic, that any true sentence is the claim, and that the claim is always first \\\\u2014 and shows each failing on the same passage. It closes by having learners pick the writer\\'s claim from a fresh short passage on their own.\"\\n    },\\n    \"neighbours\": [\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"callout\",\\n        \"reason\": \"States the sorting rule plainly \\\\u2014 a sentence counts as the claim only if the writer asserts it as true and wants the reader to accept it \\\\u2014 so the classification task has a stated criterion to apply.\",\\n        \"role\": \"sorting-rule\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"contrast-b2\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"list\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Lists the anchor passage\\'s sentences as the sorting cases for the classify-items learner action, keeping the classification on familiar ground and not outside the anchor.\",\\n        \"role\": \"anchor-sorting-cases\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"contrast-b2\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"callout\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Fails the misconception on the anchor \\\\u2014 the topic sentence names the subject but the writer is not asking the reader to accept the subject \\\\u2014 and reinstates the acceptance criterion.\",\\n        \"role\": \"anchor-failure\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"confront-b1\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n        \"reason\": \"States the belief that any true sentence is the claim and shows why it feels right, since supporting facts are true and on the same topic.\",\\n    