writer\\'s claim in a short passage by stating what the writer is asserting as true.\",\\n      \"sourcebook_entries\": [],\\n      \"subject\": \"English Language Arts\",\\n      \"teaching_plan_revision\": 1,\\n      \"title\": \"The lesson opens on the anchor passage, where one sentence is the writer\\'s claim and the others merely support it, and gives learners a reason to care about telling them apart. It builds the idea that a claim is the statement the writer asserts as true and wants readers to accept, then puts a topic sentence and a supporting fact side by side with the claim so the boundary is visible. It then surfaces the three beliefs that leak in \\\\u2014 that the claim is the topic, that any true sentence is the claim, and that the claim is always first \\\\u2014 and shows each failing on the same passage. It closes by having learners pick the writer\\'s claim from a fresh short passage on their own.\"\\n    },\\n    \"neighbours\": [\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"callout\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Fails the misconception on the anchor: the supporting fact is true and relevant, but its job is to back the claim, and the writer is not asking the reader to accept it for its own sake.\",\\n        \"role\": \"anchor-failure\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"confront-b2\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n        \"reason\": \"States the belief that the claim is always the opening sentence and shows why it feels right, since many passages announce their point first.\",\\n        \"role\": \"misconception-statement\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"confront-b3\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Sets up the fresh approved passage and states that the learner must decide which sentence states the writer\\'s claim by applying the criterion, adding no hints about position or topic.\",\\n        \"role\": \"check-framing\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"check-b1\"\\n      }\\n 