t telling them apart. It builds the idea that a claim is the statement the writer asserts as true and wants readers to accept, then puts a topic sentence and a supporting fact side by side with the claim so the boundary is visible. It then surfaces the three beliefs that leak in \\\\u2014 that the claim is the topic, that any true sentence is the claim, and that the claim is always first \\\\u2014 and shows each failing on the same passage. It closes by having learners pick the writer\\'s claim from a fresh short passage on their own.\"\\n    },\\n    \"neighbours\": [\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n        \"reason\": \"States the belief that the claim is always the opening sentence and shows why it feels right, since many passages announce their point first.\",\\n        \"role\": \"misconception-statement\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"confront-b3\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"callout\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Fails the shortcut by checking the anchor\\'s first sentence against the acceptance criterion and showing it is not the sentence the writer asks the reader to accept, then closes by restating that position cannot decide it.\",\\n        \"role\": \"anchor-failure\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"confront-b3\"\\n      }\\n    ],\\n    \"reason\": \"Sets up the fresh approved passage and states that the learner must decide which sentence states the writer\\'s claim by applying the criterion, adding no hints about position or topic.\",\\n    \"role\": \"check-framing\",\\n    \"sourcebook_entries\": [],\\n    \"teaching_block\": {\\n      \"brief\": \"Use one approved passage where the learner must decide which sentence states the writer\\'s claim. The passage is unfamiliar, so the answer must come from applying the criterion \\\\u2014 the sentence the writer asserts as true and wants accepted \\\\u2014 rather than from recalling the anchor. Do not add hints about position or topic, and do not return to the anchor pa