{"timestamp": "2026-09-16T16:42:28.709583+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "logger": "openai._base_client", "message": "Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'headers': {'User-Agent': 'pydantic-ai/1.107.1'}, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-67f466e1-4aed-4467-9bc7-b362efc2b677', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You author one already-selected educational capability. Follow the\\nsupplied AuthoringDefinition and exact output schema. Preserve block identity, objective, scope,\\nlearner action, source ownership and assessment mode. Do not select another capability or redesign\\nthe teaching plan.\\n\\nPlanning instructions are not final content. For an activity, produce a meaningful prompt, task\\ndata, justified answer relationships and useful feedback as required by the contract. Do not infer\\ncorrectness from option positions, number/word positions, example ordering or arbitrary defaults.\\nDo not copy illustrative examples as unrelated lesson content.\\n\\nTreat approved items as authoritative content. Conversion must preserve their question and answer\\nmeaning. Report incompatibility through the defined error path instead of guessing. Use supported\\nteacher-review only when the work order policy permits it.\\n\\nReturn exactly the selected output schema. Do not emit sibling schemas, markdown wrappers, internal\\nplanning metadata or extra fields. If required information is absent, use the configured\\nmissing-input mechanism; do not create placeholder content to satisfy structure.\\n\\nDo not invent or overwrite knowledge/assessment policy fields. Effective policy is code-owned.\\n\\n## KNOWLEDGE POLICY\\nKnowledge policy: supplied_preferred. Use supplied facts as the primary basis; you may add relevant model knowledge within the objective and constraints. Do not describe model knowledge as approved factual material.\\n\\n## PACKAGE CAPABILITY INSTRUCTIONS\\nCapability: classify.\\r\\n\\r\\nAuthor a complete activity envelope: student-facing prompt, classify config, and feedback.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Write the prompt as clear classification instructions the learner reads directly. The planning brief is context only \u2014 do not copy it verbatim.\\r\\n- Provide feedback.correct and feedback.incorrect messages tied to the categories and items.\\r\\n- Put categories and item assignments in config.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Declare category ids and labels before item assignments.\\r\\n- Each item belongs to exactly one category.\\r\\n- Item ids must be unique.\\r\\n- Do not use this for overlapping categories or fuzzy judgement calls.\\n\\n## AUTHORING DEFINITION\\n{\\n  \"assessment\": {\\n    \"default\": \"automatic_required\",\\n    \"supported\": [\\n      \"automatic_required\"\\n    ],\\n    \"teacher_review_permitted\": false\\n  },\\n  \"capability_id\": \"classify\",\\n  \"converter_ref\": \"learn.classify.approved_item_converter\",\\n  \"definition_hash\": \"f4fec8d3ac4c0b3628f68c9819ef918446e0e70ff2957cd7d59e48a295fd64dc\",\\n  \"instructions\": \"Capability: classify.\\\\r\\\\n\\\\r\\\\nAuthor a complete activity envelope: student-facing prompt, classify config, and feedback.\\\\r\\\\n\\\\r\\\\n- Write the prompt as clear classification instructions the learner reads directly. The planning brief is context only \\\\u2014 do not copy it verbatim.\\\\r\\\\n- Provide feedback.correct and feedback.incorrect messages tied to the categories and items.\\\\r\\\\n- Put categories and item assignments in config.\\\\r\\\\n\\\\r\\\\n- Declare category ids and labels before item assignments.\\\\r\\\\n- Each item belongs to exactly one category.\\\\r\\\\n- Item ids must be unique.\\\\r\\\\n- Do not use this for overlapping categories or fuzzy judgement calls.\",\\n  \"knowledge\": {\\n    \"default\": \"supplied_preferred\",\\n    \"supported\": [\\n      \"supplied_only\",\\n      \"supplied_preferred\",\\n      \"objective_development\"\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  \"modes\": [\\n    \"generate\",\\n    \"convert-approved\"\\n  ],\\n  \"native_path\": \"learn\",\\n  \"payload_schema\": {\\n    \"additionalProperties\": true,\\n    \"properties\": {\\n      \"config\": {\\n        \"$id\": \"learn/classify/config.v1\",\\n        \"additionalProperties\": false,\\n        \"properties\": {\\n          \"categories\": {\\n            \"items\": {\\n              \"additionalProperties\": false,\\n              \"properties\": {\\n                \"id\": {\\n                  \"minLength\": 1,\\n                  \"type\": \"string\"\\n                },\\n                \"label\": {\\n                  \"minLength\": 1,\\n                  \"type\": \"string\"\\n                }\\n              },\\n              \"required\": [\\n                \"id\",\\n                \"label\"\\n              ],\\n              \"type\": \"object\"\\n            },\\n            \"minItems\": 2,\\n            \"type\": \"array\",\\n            \"uniqueItems\": true\\n          },\\n          \"pairs\": {\\n            \"items\": {\\n              \"additionalProperties\": false,\\n              \"properties\": {\\n                \"left\": {\\n                  \"minLength\": 1,\\n                  \"type\": \"string\"\\n                },\\n                \"right\": {\\n                  \"minLength\": 1,\\n                  \"type\": \"string\"\\n                }\\n              },\\n              \"required\": [\\n                \"left\",\\n                \"right\"\\n              ],\\n              \"type\": \"object\"\\n            },\\n            \"minItems\": 1,\\n            \"type\": \"array\"\\n          }\\n        },\\n        \"required\": [\\n          \"pairs\"\\n        ],\\n        \"type\": \"object\"\\n      },\\n      \"feedback\": {\\n        \"additionalProperties\": false,\\n        \"properties\": {\\n          \"correct\": {\\n            \"type\": \"string\"\\n          },\\n          \"incorrect\": {\\n            \"type\": \"string\"\\n          },\\n          \"partial\": {\\n            \"type\": \"string\"\\n          }\\n        },\\n        \"required\": [\\n          \"correct\",\\n          \"incorrect\"\\n        ],\\n        \"type\": \"object\"\\n      },\\n      \"prompt\": {\\n        \"minLength\": 1,\\n        \"type\": \"string\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \"required\": [\\n      \"prompt\",\\n      \"config\",\\n      \"feedback\"\\n    ],\\n    \"type\": \"object\"\\n  },\\n  \"required_inputs\": [\\n    \"teaching_plan_block\",\\n    \"learn_selection_decision\",\\n    \"lesson_context\",\\n    \"allowed_facts\",\\n    \"terminology\",\\n    \"learner_action\",\\n    \"approved_items_when_converting\"\\n  ],\\n  \"validator_refs\": [\\n    \"learn.payload_schema\",\\n    \"learn.evaluateMatchPairs\"\\n  ]\\n}\\n\\n## SCOPED REQUEST\\n{\\n  \"inputs\": {\\n    \"allowed_facts\": [\\n      \"read and understand a short passage\",\\n      \"find details in a text\",\\n      \"A claim is what the writer asserts as true.\",\\n      \"The claim can be stated in a single sentence.\",\\n      \"Recognizing a writer\\'s claim in a short passage\",\\n      \"Distinguishing evidence that directly supports a claim from unrelated details\",\\n      \"Explaining the link between selected evidence and the claim\"\\n    ],\\n    \"approved_items_when_converting\": [],\\n    \"learn_selection_decision\": {\\n      \"capability_id\": \"classify\",\\n      \"lane\": \"interaction\",\\n      \"support_level\": null\\n    },\\n    \"learner_action\": \"classify-items\",\\n    \"lesson_context\": {\\n      \"input_availability\": \"supplied_statements\",\\n      \"objective\": \"Identify the writer\\'s claim in a short passage by stating what the writer is asserting as true.\",\\n      \"sourcebook_entries\": [],\\n      \"subject\": \"English Language Arts\",\\n      \"teaching_plan_revision\": 1,\\n      \"title\": \"The lesson opens on the anchor passage, where one sentence is the writer\\'s claim and the others merely support it, and gives learners a reason to care about telling them apart. It builds the idea that a claim is the statement the writer asserts as true and wants readers to accept, then puts a topic sentence and a supporting fact side by side with the claim so the boundary is visible. It then surfaces the three beliefs that leak in \\\\u2014 that the claim is the topic, that any true sentence is the claim, and that the claim is always first \\\\u2014 and shows each failing on the same passage. It closes by having learners pick the writer\\'s claim from a fresh short passage on their own.\"\\n    },\\n    \"shared_task\": {\\n      \"action\": \"classify-items\",\\n      \"approved_source_ids\": [],\\n      \"difficulty\": \"guided\",\\n      \"evaluation\": {\\n        \"criteria\": [\\n          \"the claim sentence placed correctly and each non-claim sentence placed with its role \\\\u2014 topic or support \\\\u2014 distinguishable\"\\n        ],\\n        \"type\": \"rubric\"\\n      },\\n      \"expected_evidence\": \"the claim sentence placed correctly and each non-claim sentence placed with its role \\\\u2014 topic or support \\\\u2014 distinguishable\",\\n      \"feedback\": null,\\n      \"id\": \"task-contrast-b2\",\\n      \"mode\": \"formative\",\\n      \"prompt\": \"Give the rule for sorting sentences: a sentence belongs to the writer\\'s claim only if the writer is asserting it as true and wants the reader to accept it; a sentence that names the subject or gives a supporting reason is not the claim. Use the anchor passage\\'s sentences as the sorting cases and have the learner place each one, guided by the rule just stated. Do not use sentences from outside the anchor here \\\\u2014 the sorting is on familiar ground.\",\\n      \"purpose\": \"require the learner to apply the acceptance criterion to real candidate sentences before the misconceptions are named\",\\n      \"response\": {\\n        \"type\": \"classification\"\\n      },\\n      \"sourcebook_refs\": [],\\n      \"teaching_block_id\": \"contrast-b2\",\\n      \"teaching_plan_hash\": \"a9530874d702efc409dcd4c87658fe037981b8a8a6ed76e1d95a551ff8885870\",\\n      \"teaching_plan_id\": \"86096efd-501d-45d0-9b33-7101fd652b6a\",\\n      \"teaching_plan_revision\": 1\\n    },\\n    \"teaching_plan_block\": {\\n      \"action\": \"classify-items\",\\n      \"block_id\": \"contrast-b2\",\\n      \"brief\": \"Give the rule for sorting sentences: a sentence belongs to the writer\\'s claim only if the writer is asserting it as true and wants the reader to accept it; a sentence that names the subject or gives a supporting reason is not the claim. Use the anchor passage\\'s sentences as the sorting cases and have the learner place each one, guided by the rule just stated. Do not use sentences from outside the anchor here \\\\u2014 the sorting is on familiar ground.\",\\n      \"evidence\": \"Classification with a stated rule is the move that turns the comparison from something the teacher did into something the learner can do; it belongs after the side-by-side comparison so the rule has visible cases to bite on.\",\\n      \"intent\": \"classify\",\\n      \"section_id\": \"contrast\"\\n    },\\n    \"terminology\": [\\n      \"claim\",\\n      \"evidence\",\\n      \"relevant evidence\",\\n      \"irrelevant detail\",\\n      \"support\"\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  \"mode\": \"generate\",\\n  \"resolved_policy\": {\\n    \"assessment_policy_source\": \"package:1.0.0\",\\n    \"definition_hash\": \"f4fec8d3ac4c0b3628f68c9819ef918446e0e70ff2957cd7d59e48a295fd64dc\",\\n    \"definition_version\": \"2.0.0\",\\n    \"effective_assessment_policy\": \"automatic_required\",\\n    \"effective_knowledge_policy\": \"supplied_preferred\",\\n    \"executed_evaluation_mode\": \"accepted-answers\",\\n    \"executed_knowledge_mode\": \"supplied_facts\",\\n    \"fallback_reason\": null,\\n    \"input_availability